[{"id":15,"slug":"itb-shop-growth-potential","theme":"Data Analytics","title":"ITB Round 3 (Final) – Shop Growth Potential","category":"Sales Network Analytics · Forecasting","award":"Runner-up of 200 teams","description":"Two partner shops can sign the same number of loan contracts today, yet one still has room to grow and the other is already at its limit. For the final round of the ITB Competition, where our team finished runner-up of 200, we estimated four levels for each of a lender's 11,795 shops (today, business as usual, attainable with feasible actions, and the market ceiling) and showed Sales Management which shops to invest in, what that returns, and how to prove it.","tags":["Python","DuckDB","SQL","pandas","LightGBM","scikit-learn","Quantile Regression","Forecasting","Sales Analytics"],"link":"#","github":"https://github.com/AIVIETNAM-AIO-thuhuynhtran05/ITB-Shop-Growth-Potential","presentation":{"pdf":"/slides/itb-final-shop-growth-potential-slides.pdf","slides":["/slides/itb-r3/slide-01.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-02.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-03.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-04.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-05.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-06.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-07.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-08.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-09.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-10.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-11.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-12.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-13.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-14.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-15.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-16.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-17.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-18.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-19.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-20.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-21.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-22.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-23.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-24.jpg","/slides/itb-r3/slide-25.jpg"]},"image":"/images/projects/itb-shop-growth-potential.svg","related":{"label":"Round 2 of the competition","title":"Customer Financial Health & Engagement Scoring","text":"The final round continued the storyline of Round 2, where we scored 1.0M customers on financial health and engagement and gave each one a next best action. Those scores, set in June 2025, still ranked customers correctly on 16 months of new data in this round: Champions signed a new contract 40% of the time against 5% for Dormant customers.","href":"/projects/itb-customer-health-engagement-scoring","cta":"See Round 2"},"facts":[{"value":"11,795","label":"Partner shops assessed"},{"value":"41.5K","label":"Contracts a month (Q3/2026)"},{"value":"17","label":"Forecasting methods backtested"},{"value":"57%","label":"Of the market ceiling in use"}],"details":{"challenge":{"label":"The Problem","title":"Same Sales, Different Headroom","summary":"The final round of the ITB Competition (Business Intelligence Season 10, UEL) asked how far a specific shop can realistically grow, what constrains it, and what Sales Management should do to close the gap. Shops are partner stores where customers apply for loans in person; a shop can also pass on a lead that is later signed online, so the shop that books a contract is not always the one that found the customer. Teams had to keep four levels of performance apart, continue the storyline from Round 2, and defend the answer in 10 minutes of presentation and 15 minutes of judges' questions.","points":["<strong>Current Actual:</strong> what the shop achieves today. The network signs 41.5K contracts and VND 539 bn a month across 5,331 active shops, 2.4× more shops than a year earlier. But 93% of growth comes from opening shops: contracts per shop are flat, and 51% of shops sign less than one contract a month.","<strong>Business-as-Usual:</strong> what the shop reaches if nothing changes.","<strong>Attainable Potential:</strong> what feasible actions within Sales' control can add.","<strong>Addressable Market Ceiling:</strong> the demand that could in theory be served.","<strong>If two shops sell the same today, which one can grow?</strong> The brief asked teams to answer this for Sales Management, and to say which shops should get more resources, how much they would return, and why."]},"solution":{"summary":"One clean shop table, then one model per level. Each level has its own phase below, and BAU ≤ Attainable ≤ Ceiling holds for every shop.","steps":["Prepared the data in DuckDB SQL and pandas: removed 208,991 duplicate lead rows, weighted the down-sampled leads instead of counting rows, mapped 54 province names to the 34 provinces after the 2025 merger, and converted shared sales-agent (SA) assignments to full-time equivalents, since raw headcount overstated staffing about 25×.","Linked leads to contracts with the brief's three rules (same client, same product, lead assigned before the application), one-to-one within 30 days, so an online contract started by a shop's lead is credited back to that shop and counted only once.","Built one row per shop with 187 columns: funnel, products, partner, staffing, market, and Round 2 customer labels.","Estimated the four levels in order (Current Actual, BAU, Attainable, Ceiling), then turned the gaps between them into a bottleneck and a priority tier for every shop.","Checked reliability with backtests, sensitivity tests, and bootstrap ranges, and sized a pilot to prove the uplift in practice."]},"architecture":{"flow":["Contracts, leads, shops, staff (DuckDB)","Clean & link lead → contract","Current Actual","BAU forecast","Attainable (measured levers)","Market Ceiling","Bottlenecks & priority tiers"],"before":["Shops were ranked by current sales: 20% of active shops make 68% of contracts, but the ranking says nothing about how far a shop can still go.","Size, model, and region labels each explain at most 5% of the difference between shops, so one target per label is too high for some shops and too low for others."],"after":["Every shop has four numbers (today, BAU with a P10–P90 range, attainable, and ceiling), its main bottleneck, and a priority tier.","Sales Management can fund shops in priority order, see the expected return, and test it in a controlled pilot before scaling."]},"eda":{"summary":"Raw data would have misstated shop volume, lead counts, and staffing, so we fixed that first, then described how the network grows and where customers drop out of the funnel.","facts":[{"label":"Data","value":"Contracts, products, leads, shops, partners, and staff assignments from 31/12/2025 to 17/10/2026. Current quarter Q3/2026, forecast quarter Q4/2026. 11,795 shops: 5,331 active, 4,304 newly opened, and 2,160 inactive."},{"label":"Data quality","value":"1.05M unsigned rows carry the placeholder date 3000-01-01; first-payment default on contracts too recent to mature would read as about 0%, so risk is measured on mature contracts only; goods groups had spelling variants (21 merged into 14)."},{"label":"Funnel","value":"Of 100 applications, 79 are approved, 72 are signed, and 64 pay the first instalment on time. Only 29% of leads ever reach an application."},{"label":"Cross-sell","value":"Shops make 28% of contracts but 19% of value, since large cash loans close online. 83% of shop customers are new to the lender, and cross-sell is only 6.1% of shop contracts."}],"visuals":[{"image":"/slides/itb-r3/slide-05.jpg","width":1920,"height":1080,"alt":"Data issues in the contract, product, lead, lead outcome, shop, partner, and staff tables, with the fix and the check applied to each","caption":"Data issues by table and how each was fixed: no contract or shop rows dropped, 208,991 duplicate lead rows removed, provinces mapped from 54 names to 34, and staffing measured in full-time equivalents."},{"image":"/slides/itb-r3/slide-06.jpg","width":1920,"height":1080,"alt":"Network growth split into new shops versus output per shop, contracts per shop over time, and the lead and application funnel","caption":"The network grows by adding shops, not by better shops: 93% of new contracts come from new shops, and output stays flat at about 3 contracts per shop a month."}]},"models":[{"label":"Levels 1–2","title":"Current Actual & Business-as-Usual","question":"What does the shop sign today, and what will it sign next quarter if nothing changes?","summary":"Current Actual is measured directly. BAU is a forecast: we tested 17 methods by forecasting past quarters with only the data known at the time, and kept the one that was accurate and fair to every group of shops.","steps":["<strong>Measure today.</strong> Signed contracts a month in Q3/2026, counted by the quarter the application was filed, so applications × approval rate × closing rate add up exactly. Value is reported alongside as risk-adjusted lending: amount lent × (1 − first-payment default rate).","<strong>Credit the shop that found the customer.</strong> An online contract that started from a shop's lead is moved back to that shop, and each contract is counted once in the network total.","<strong>Backtest 17 methods.</strong> Each method forecasts Q1, Q2, and Q3 2026 from earlier data only. The winner is judged on error per shop and on bias by shop group: no group may be off by more than 20%.","<strong>Blend three models.</strong> The chosen method is the average of LightGBM, Random Forest, and exponential smoothing on each shop's recent results. Averaging cancels their separate blind spots; \"keep as is\" alone underestimated low-volume shops by 64%.","<strong>Forecast contracts first, money second.</strong> One large motorbike loan can swing a small shop's quarter, so volume is forecast first and the average loan size is applied after, with its upward trend by segment (backtested against holding it flat or damping it).","<strong>Give every shop a range.</strong> P10–P90 bands combine one shock shared by all shops with each shop's own backtest error; 78–82% of shop actuals fell inside their band (target 80%).","<strong>Explain each number.</strong> Shapley values show recent results drive the forecast: last month (+39%) and the current-quarter level (+29%), while age, size, and partner type add almost nothing."],"visuals":[{"image":"/slides/itb-r3/slide-12.jpg","width":1920,"height":1080,"alt":"BAU forecast: factor importance of the blended model, network contracts per month from 2025 to Q4/2026 with the P10–P90 range, and backtest coverage","caption":"Q4/2026 BAU is 41,186 contracts a month against 41,467 today (−0.7%), within about −5% to +7%. Value rises about 4% only because loans keep getting larger."}]},{"label":"Level 3","title":"Attainable Potential","question":"What can feasible actions within Sales' control add?","summary":"We compare each shop with shops that work under the same conditions to see how far behind it is, but count only the part that a measured lever can close. The peer level shows the size of the gap; the lever shows what is realistic.","steps":["<strong>Form peer groups.</strong> Shops are grouped by product line, operating model (Aldi or Non-Aldi), size, and partner type. If a group has fewer than 30 active shops, the shop falls back to a broader group (line × model × size, then model × size, then model).","<strong>Size the gap.</strong> The 75th percentile (P75) of the peer group is the reference \"good shop\". The distance to it shows how far behind a shop is, not how much it will gain: taken at face value it would imply +52%.","<strong>Measure each lever.</strong> Track the same shop across two quarters, net of the change its whole segment saw, to see what one more unit of a resource delivers.","<strong>Count only what closes.</strong> Each lever closes part of the gap at the rate seen in history. Low, mid, and high scenarios assume the shop improves like the best 40%, 25%, or 10% of shops did. Closing gaps on approved applications mostly close on their own (82–100%), so only the remainder counts.","<strong>Recalculate the funnel.</strong> Extra applications still pass through approval and closing, and the total can never exceed the gap to P75 or the shop's market ceiling.","<strong>Apply the risk guardrail.</strong> For the 465 shops whose first-payment default or delinquency is above their peer P90, Attainable = BAU."],"tables":[{"caption":"Levers and their measured effect","columns":["Lever","Measured effect","Owner","In Attainable?","Adds (contracts a month)"],"rows":[["More campaign leads","+1 lead a month → +0.58 contracts (Aldi), +0.22 (Non-Aldi)","Sales / marketing","Yes","+1,344"],["One more sales agent","+1 SA → +0.52 contracts, Aldi shops only","Regional manager","Yes","+174"],["Better lead follow-up","Close part of the gap to peer P75 in lead conversion","Sales manager","Yes","+54"],["Closing coaching, missing products","Gaps mostly close on their own","Sales / product","Yes","≈ 0"],["Looser approval","+1,501 contracts but ~303 early defaults","Risk team","No, scenario only","–"],["Cross-sell CL / CC","+322 contracts credited to shops","Sales","No, reported separately","–"]]}],"visuals":[{"image":"/slides/itb-r3/slide-15.jpg","width":1920,"height":1080,"alt":"Waterfall from BAU 25,780 to Attainable 27,354 contracts a month for active shops by lever, and low, mid, and high Attainable scenarios against the peer P75 level","caption":"Active shops go from BAU 25,780 to Attainable 27,354 contracts a month (+6.1%, range 26,401–29,177), 85% of it from campaign leads."}]},{"label":"Level 4","title":"Addressable Market Ceiling","question":"How much suitable loan demand could the shops serve at most?","summary":"The ceiling is built by province as a chain of rates, then shared among the shops in each province. It keeps today's products and credit policy, so it measures demand, not a wish list.","steps":["<strong>Start with people.</strong> Population of each of the 34 provinces from the 2024 mid-term census.","<strong>Estimate demand per person.</strong> Applications per 100K people that provinces with similar income and density already reach. A quantile regression across 34 provinces, log(applications per 100K) ~ log(income) + log(density), gives the 75th percentile (low scenario) and 90th (base). Provinces already above that keep their actual level.","<strong>Apply today's policy.</strong> Multiply by the province's approval rate and its signed-to-approved rate, so the ceiling is in the same unit as BAU and Attainable.","<strong>Add a high scenario.</strong> Base ÷ 50%, assuming the lender currently wins half of the instalment demand at its partner shops (sensitivity 30–70%).","<strong>Share it among shops.</strong> Each province's ceiling is split by shop characteristics (peer group, partner chain size, partner age, order value), never by the shop's own sales.","<strong>Cross-check.</strong> Leads outside the RTDM campaigns (45,311 a month) × the peer P90 contracts-per-lead rate gives 71,965, close to the base ceiling of 72,242."],"tables":[{"caption":"Ceiling scenarios (contracts a month, network)","columns":["Scenario","Demand benchmark","Contracts","Value"],"rows":[["Low","Provinces at P75 of peers","54,701","VND 754 bn"],["Base","Provinces at P90 of peers","72,242","VND 1,000 bn"],["High","Base ÷ 50% market share","144,483","VND 2,000 bn"]]}],"visuals":[{"image":"/slides/itb-r3/slide-16.jpg","width":1920,"height":1080,"alt":"How the market ceiling is built as a chain of rates, loan demand by province against income, the top provinces by uncaptured ceiling, and active shops from BAU to ceiling","caption":"The network uses about 57% of its base ceiling. Income raises demand less than in proportion (elasticity 0.91 at P90), so population × income would overstate it."}]},{"label":"Groups","title":"From Gaps to Bottlenecks and Priority Tiers","question":"Why is a shop behind, and where should the next unit of resource go?","summary":"The four levels give each shop two gaps: Attainable − BAU is the opportunity actions can close, and Ceiling − Attainable is the market it has not reached. Simple rules turn those gaps into a bottleneck, a headroom class, and a priority tier.","steps":["<strong>Name the bottleneck.</strong> The lever with the largest gap to peers: short of customers (applications below attainable), weak closing (closing rate below peer P75), missing products (at least half of peers sell a product the shop does not), or no sales agent (a Non-Aldi shop with none assigned).","<strong>Classify headroom.</strong> Near ceiling if the opportunity is under 0.5 contracts a month or under 15%; moderate at 0.5–2; large at 2 or more.","<strong>Assign a tier.</strong> Inactive shops are tier D and shops opened recently are tracked separately. Active shops with moderate or large headroom are tier A; shops near their ceiling are tier B if they sell 3 or more contracts a month and tier C otherwise.","<strong>Score priority.</strong> Value uplift reachable in 6 months (at most two quarters of the growth the top 20% of similar shops achieved) × (1 − penalty). The penalty is 0.5 when first-payment default or delinquency is above the peer P90, and 0.2 when the shop has fewer than 10 mature contracts.","<strong>Fund in order.</strong> Rank tier-A shops by score and add them until the extra value per shop flattens."],"tables":[{"caption":"Priority tiers for all 11,795 shops","columns":["Tier","Rule","Shops","Action"],"rows":[["A","Active, moderate or large headroom","3,954","Invest, in priority order"],["B","Active, near ceiling, 3+ contracts a month","1,261","Maintain"],["C","Active, near ceiling, under 3 contracts a month","116","Monitor"],["New","Opened recently","4,304","Track separately"],["D","No contract in the whole quarter","2,160","Reactivate 696, exit 1,464"]]}],"visuals":[{"image":"/slides/itb-r3/slide-17.jpg","width":1920,"height":1080,"alt":"Main bottleneck per shop, strong versus weak shops by funnel step, SA staffing against sales, and the two-shop case study","caption":"Two shops at about 4 contracts a month in the same peer group: #117673569 can add +1.8 a month because it is short of customers (target 9 applications, a 5-shop partner chain, no credit card or BNPL yet), while #96605617 is near its limit."},{"image":"/slides/itb-r3/slide-18.jpg","width":1920,"height":1080,"alt":"All 11,795 shops by tier, the risk-adjusted scoring rule, and the curve of extra value as more tier-A shops are funded","caption":"Funding the top 500 tier-A shops returns about +392 contracts and +VND 6.4 bn a month, around 60% of the value of the top 1,000 for half the shops."}]}],"implementation":{"title":"Reliability & Tools","facts":[{"label":"Backtest of headroom","value":"Rerunning everything with data up to Q1/2026: among shops selling under 3 contracts a month, those flagged with headroom then grew by +0.41 to +0.45 contracts a month while near-ceiling shops fell by 0.24."},{"label":"Sensitivity","value":"Moving the peer benchmark to P70 or P80, or the ceiling from P90 to P75, keeps 87–96% of the top 1,000 shops. A 90% bootstrap range for the P75 reference is 38,824–39,611 contracts a month."},{"label":"Pilot design","value":"About 1,270 shops per arm for 3 months detects the mid-scenario uplift of 0.42 contracts a month per shop; any shop whose first-payment default rises above the peer P90 is stopped."},{"label":"Bottlenecks today","value":"Short of customers: 3,561 active shops · near ceiling: 1,377 · weak closing: 350 · missing products: 43. Assigned SA headcount barely relates to sales (r = 0.02–0.05)."},{"label":"External data","value":"Population (2024) and average income (2025) for the 34 provinces from Vietnam's National Statistics Office, matched 34/34 to shop provinces, with source, coverage, mapping, and limits documented."},{"label":"Tools","value":"DuckDB SQL and pandas for data; LightGBM and scikit-learn for the BAU forecast and quantile regression; SciPy and NumPy for bootstrap and pilot sizing. ITB Competition final round, October 2026 · runner-up of 200 teams."}]},"results":{"outcomesTitle":"Key Insights","validationTitle":"Recommendations","outcomes":["No action means no growth: Q4/2026 BAU is 41,186 contracts a month (−0.7%), with a range of about ±7%.","Weak shops lack customers, not skills: strong shops get about 8× more applications than weak ones at nearly the same approval (×1.02) and completion (×0.95) rates, and the whole 1,574-contract gap between BAU and Attainable comes from too few applications.","Benchmarks overstate potential: taking the peer P75 at face value implies +52%, while measured levers support +6.1%.","Market size is not the limit for most shops: the network uses about 57% of its base ceiling. Hà Nội uses 37% and Hải Phòng 32%, while Đồng Nai is already at 100%.","Same sales, different headroom: among shops selling 3–10 contracts a month, 764 are near their ceiling and 238 still have large room."],"validation":["Invest in the 3,954 tier-A shops ranked by risk-adjusted headroom. Start with the top 500 for about +392 contracts and +VND 6.4 bn a month (range 2.6–13.8 bn), then extend to 1,000–2,000 shops.","Route about 21.8K campaign leads a quarter to shops below their peer P75, matched by product and location, with same-day contact rules; add SAs only at under-staffed Aldi shops, and coach lead handling where closing is weak.","Set no growth targets for the 465 high-risk shops, and leave approval relaxation to the Risk team, since it adds volume but also bad debt.","Reactivate 696 inactive shops with willing partners and strong local demand, exit 1,464, and expand in under-served provinces such as Hà Nội, Hải Phòng, Ninh Bình, and Hưng Yên.","Prove it with a 3-month controlled pilot of about 1,270 shops per arm before scaling."]}}},{"id":1,"slug":"vin-datathon","theme":"Data Analytics","title":"VinDatathon 2026 – E-commerce Business Analytics","category":"Data Competition · Retail & E-commerce","description":"A Vietnamese fashion e-commerce business grew until 2016, then shrank for six years even though more and more people visited its website. Using 10.5 years of its data, we traced what went wrong and why, found which customers bring the revenue and which have slipped away, and proposed what to fix first, with a sales forecast to plan ahead.","tags":["Power BI","DAX","Python","pandas","SQL","K-Means","RFM Segmentation","Data Quality","E-commerce Analytics"],"link":"#","github":"https://github.com/AIVIETNAM-AIO-thuhuynhtran05/Vin-Datathon","powerBiProject":"https://github.com/AIVIETNAM-AIO-thuhuynhtran05/Vin-Datathon/tree/main/dashboard","powerBiDownload":"/downloads/vin-datathon-sale-analytics-pbip.zip","image":"/images/projects/vin-datathon.svg","related":{"label":"Part 3 of the competition","title":"Sales Forecasting","text":"The same round also asked teams to forecast daily Revenue and COGS for the next 548 days (01/01/2023 – 01/07/2024). A Ridge and LightGBM ensemble built from calendar features reached R² 0.807 on 2022 and shows revenue holding flat at about 1.18B VND in 2023. The steps and results are covered as a separate project.","href":"/projects/vin-datathon-sales-forecasting","cta":"See the forecasting project"},"facts":[{"value":"15","label":"CSV files in 4 data layers"},{"value":"647K","label":"Orders"},{"value":"122K","label":"Customers"},{"value":"10.5 years","label":"Jul 2012 – Dec 2022"}],"details":{"challenge":{"label":"The Problem","title":"The Problem & Business Questions","summary":"The business peaked around 2016, then declined for six years: revenue fell at −3.8% a year and margin dropped from 22% to 14%, even though website traffic kept growing. Leadership needed to know why, who to focus on, what to expect next, and what to fix first. The competition judged answers on four levels, from \"what happened\" to \"what should we do\", which gives five business questions.","points":["<strong>What happened?</strong> How have revenue, orders, margin, customers, and traffic moved since 2012, and where exactly did the decline start?","<strong>Why did it happen?</strong> Is the decline caused by too little traffic, poor conversion, customers not coming back, discounts, inventory, or delivery and returns?","<strong>Who should we act on?</strong> Which customer groups bring the revenue, which have gone quiet, and which never bought at all?","<strong>What is likely to happen next?</strong> What will daily revenue be from January 2023 to July 2024, so stock, promotions, and logistics can be planned?","<strong>What should we do?</strong> Which actions to take first, for which customers, and which KPI shows whether each one works?"]},"solution":{"label":"Project Structure","title":"Project Structure","summary":"The project has five parts. Trusted data feeds three analyses, each answering one business question, and their results come together in the recommendations. Each part is covered in its own section below.","visuals":[{"image":"/images/projects/vin/vin-project-structure.svg?v=3","width":1000,"height":640,"alt":"Project structure: Part 1 data foundation feeds Part 2 business dashboard, Part 3 customer segmentation, and Part 4 sales forecasting, which all feed Part 5 recommendations and KPIs; each part lists the question it answers and its business output","caption":"Each part answers one business question and produces an output the business can use."}]},"architecture":{"label":"Part 1 · Data Quality","title":"Data Quality & EDA","flow":["13 tables · 2.96M rows","362 automated checks (Python)","2 errors · 12 warnings","A decision for each issue","EDA on every column","Clean base for the analysis"],"beforeTitle":"Issues found","before":["Keys, foreign keys, and payment totals were clean: no duplicate primary keys, and every payment matches its order lines.","Refunds were higher than the amount paid on 3,633 orders, because refunds were calculated before discounts.","564 orders marked shipped, delivered, or returned had no shipment record.","73.8% of orders were placed before the customer's sign-up date, so sign-up dates cannot be trusted.","EDA: order values are heavily right-skewed (4.7% of payments are outliers), 38.7% of order lines use a promotion, and 814 of 2,412 products never sold."],"afterTitle":"How they were handled","after":["Refunds were capped at the amount paid, so net revenue is never negative.","Orders without a shipment record were kept for revenue but left out of delivery-time analysis.","Customer tenure starts from the first order instead of the sign-up date.","Skewed values were log-transformed for segmentation instead of dropping outliers, and products that never sold were left out of product performance.","Created and cancelled orders (66,737, 10.3%) bring in no revenue, so they were left out of revenue, AOV, and margin, and kept only to measure the cancel rate."]},"kpis":{"label":"Part 2 · Business Dashboard","title":"KPIs, Data Model & Pages","summary":"The completed PBIP report has five interactive pages, 46 DAX measures, and slicers for the relevant business dimensions. The Executive Overview replaces single-value KPI cards with yearly trend lines for Revenue, Gross Profit, Gross Margin, Orders, AOV, and YoY Revenue Growth, making the decade-long direction visible at a glance. A KPI tree ties the analysis together: Revenue = Sessions × Conversion Rate × AOV, and Gross Profit = Revenue × Gross Margin %. Sales KPIs count only revenue-generating orders (paid, shipped, delivered, returned); created and cancelled orders are included only in the cancel rate.","root":{"title":"Power BI Dashboard","subtitle":"5 interactive pages · 46 DAX KPIs"},"modelTitle":"From raw data to the model","modelSummary":"A Python (pandas) script cleans, enriches, and reshapes the 13 raw CSV files into a star schema of 15 tables: 5 dimensions, 7 fact tables, and 3 pre-aggregated tables, each at one grain so nothing is counted twice. The committed PBIP project includes the Power BI relationships and 46 DAX measures in its _KPI table.","stepLabel":"Step","steps":["Load the 13 raw CSV files and keep them read-only. Some files store dates as dd/mm/yyyy and others as yyyy-mm-dd, so the script detects the format per column.","Build the dimensions: a calendar table; products with margin, average rating, and return rate; customers with region, RFM scores and labels, cohort month, and their Part 3 segment.","Build the facts, each at one grain: orders (payment, shipping fee, refund, new vs repeat), order lines (revenue, discount, cost, gross profit), returns, shipments (days to ship and deliver), daily sales (MA7/MA30, YoY), monthly inventory (stock value, health label), and daily web traffic (conversion rate).","Count only revenue orders (paid, shipped, delivered, returned) for RFM, cohorts, new vs repeat, and conversion rate. Fact tables keep every status with an is_revenue_order flag, so the cancel rate can still be measured. Order-line revenue reconciles exactly with sales.csv (16.43B).","Pre-aggregate what is slow or awkward in DAX: a monthly KPI rollup with MoM/YoY growth, a cohort retention matrix (months 0–24), and the rating trend by month × category. The result is 15 CSV files, including 714,669 order lines and 646,945 orders.","Model in Power BI: mark the date dimension as the date table, use one-to-many single-direction relationships from dimensions to facts (fact tables never link to each other, so every filter has one path), and keep KPI measures in the _KPI table."],"visuals":[{"image":"/images/projects/vin/vin-star-schema.svg?v=4","width":1240,"height":730,"alt":"Power BI star schema: a date dimension on top filters seven fact tables (web traffic, daily sales, orders, order lines, returns, shipments, inventory), which are filtered below by geography, customers, promotions, and products dimensions; three pre-aggregated tables stand alone","caption":"The Power BI star schema: seven fact tables, each at its own grain, filtered by a date dimension and four other dimensions, plus three pre-aggregated tables for monthly KPIs, cohort retention, and ratings. Fact tables never link to each other."}],"dashboard":{"title":"The dashboard","summary":"Five pages, each with its own slicers and six headline KPIs, going from the overall trend down to customers, products, inventory, and logistics.","visuals":[{"image":"/images/projects/vin/vin-dashboard-overview.png","width":1984,"height":1142,"alt":"Executive Overview page: yearly trend cards for revenue, gross profit, gross margin, orders, AOV, and YoY growth; monthly revenue and gross profit; gross profit by category; website sessions; conversion rate; and a financial table by category","caption":"Executive Overview: sessions keep rising while the conversion rate falls from about 1% to under 0.5% after 2019, which is why revenue shrinks. Streetwear brings 1.05bn of the 1.36bn gross profit."},{"image":"/images/projects/vin/vin-dashboard-customer.png","width":1984,"height":1132,"alt":"Customer Insights page: KPI cards for 122K customers, 72.04% buyer rate, 75.23% repeat rate, 4.76 purchase frequency, 115.25K customer LTV, and 6.22% month +1 retention; customers by RFM segment, age group, and gender; tables of value by segment and demographics","caption":"Customer Insights: 72% of registered customers buy and 75% of buyers come back, but only 6.22% buy again the next month. Champions average 15 orders and 369K lifetime value, while 31.7K customers never purchased."},{"image":"/images/projects/vin/vin-dashboard-product.png","width":1942,"height":1142,"alt":"Product & Promotion page: KPI cards for 3M units sold, 14.05bn revenue, 1.36bn gross profit, 9.69% margin, 521 loss-making products, and 38.35% promo share; best-selling and lowest-profit products; revenue and profit by category; promotional vs non-promotional AOV; product profitability table","caption":"Product & Promotion: a 9.69% gross margin with 521 loss-making products. Orders without a promotion have a higher AOV (about 27K) than fixed or percentage promotions (about 18–19K)."},{"image":"/images/projects/vin/vin-dashboard-inventory.png","width":1990,"height":1102,"alt":"Inventory page: KPI cards for 104K stock on hand, 14.1 days of supply, 67.34% stockout rate, 76.26% overstock rate, 89.91% sell-through, and 90.32% slow stock rate; stock on hand trend; on-hand units and days of supply by category; stock by inventory health; inventory by product table","caption":"Inventory: stock on hand has doubled since 2014 and stays near 100K, yet 67% of product-months run out of stock and 76% are overstocked. Stock sits in the wrong products rather than being too high or too low overall."},{"image":"/images/projects/vin/vin-dashboard-logistics.png","width":1894,"height":1114,"alt":"Logistics & Returns page: KPI cards for 4.5 delivery days, 1.5 order-to-ship days, 566K shipment orders, 9.19% cancel rate, 6.99% return rate, and 2.81M shipping cost; delivery and processing time by region; shipments by delivery time band; returns by reason; wrong-size return share by category; logistics table by region","caption":"Logistics & Returns: delivery takes 4.5 days in every region, so speed is not the problem. Wrong size is by far the top return reason, at about 35% of returns in every category."}],"actionsTitle":"What each page says to do","actions":[{"page":"Executive Overview","action":"Fix conversion before buying more traffic. Sessions kept rising while conversion fell below 0.5%, so audit the checkout funnel (payment, price, delivery fee) and A/B test each fix.","track":"Conversion rate by month"},{"page":"Customer Insights","action":"Win the second order. Only 6.22% of customers buy again the next month, so send a second-order offer within 30 days of the first order and an onboarding flow to the 31.7K who signed up but never bought.","track":"Month +1 retention, buyer rate"},{"page":"Product & Promotion","action":"Reprice or delist the 521 loss-making products, starting with the lowest gross profit, and cut back blanket promotions: promoted orders average about 18–19K against 27K without one.","track":"Loss-making products, gross margin %"},{"page":"Inventory","action":"Rebalance stock by product instead of adding more: 67% of product-months run out while 76% are overstocked. Reorder from product-level demand and clear overstock through bundles and markdowns.","track":"Stockout rate, overstock rate"},{"page":"Logistics & Returns","action":"Leave delivery speed alone (4.5 days everywhere) and attack wrong-size returns, about 35% of returns in every category, with size guides, measurements, and fit labels.","track":"Return rate, wrong-size share"}]},"groups":[{"title":"Executive Overview","question":"Is the business growing or shrinking, and why?","items":[{"name":"Revenue","formula":"Sessions × CR × AOV"},{"name":"Gross Profit","formula":"Revenue × Gross Margin %"},{"name":"Orders","full":"Paid, shipped, delivered, returned","formula":"Count of revenue orders"},{"name":"Customers","formula":"Count of buyers with a revenue order"},{"name":"CR","full":"Conversion Rate","formula":"Orders ÷ Sessions"},{"name":"YoY growth","formula":"(This year − Last year) ÷ Last year"}]},{"title":"Customer Insights","question":"Do customers buy, and do they come back?","items":[{"name":"Buyer rate","full":"Registered customers who bought","formula":"Customers with a completed order ÷ Registered customers"},{"name":"Repeat rate","formula":"Customers with 2+ orders ÷ Customers"},{"name":"Month +1 retention","full":"Cohort by first-order month","formula":"Cohort buyers in month +1 ÷ Cohort size"},{"name":"LTV","full":"Customer Lifetime Value","formula":"Revenue ÷ Customers"},{"name":"Recency","formula":"Today − Last order date"},{"name":"RFM segment","formula":"K-Means on Recency, Frequency, Monetary"}]},{"title":"Product & Promotion","question":"Which products and categories drive revenue and profit, and do promotions pay off?","items":[{"name":"AOV","full":"Average Order Value","formula":"Revenue ÷ Orders"},{"name":"Gross Margin %","formula":"Gross profit ÷ Revenue"},{"name":"Promo share","formula":"Promo orders ÷ Orders"},{"name":"Promo AOV gap","formula":"AOV with promo − AOV without promo"},{"name":"Discount rate","formula":"Discount amount ÷ Revenue before discount"},{"name":"Cart Size","full":"Items per order","formula":"Units sold ÷ Orders"}]},{"title":"Inventory Health","question":"Is stock in balance with demand?","items":[{"name":"DOS","full":"Days of Supply","formula":"Stock on hand ÷ Avg daily units sold"},{"name":"R/S ratio","full":"Receive-to-Sales","formula":"Units received ÷ Units sold"},{"name":"Stockout rate","formula":"Out-of-stock product-months ÷ All product-months"},{"name":"Overstock rate","formula":"Overstocked product-months ÷ All product-months"},{"name":"Sell-through","formula":"Units sold ÷ Units available"},{"name":"Slow stock","full":"Stock with DOS over 365 days","formula":"Stock with DOS > 365 ÷ Stock on hand"}]},{"title":"Logistics & Returns","question":"Do orders arrive, and do they stay?","items":[{"name":"Delivery days","formula":"Avg (Delivery date − Order date)"},{"name":"Order-to-ship","formula":"Avg (Ship date − Order date)"},{"name":"Cancel rate","formula":"Cancelled orders ÷ All orders placed"},{"name":"Return rate","formula":"Returned orders ÷ Delivered orders"},{"name":"Wrong Size share","formula":"Wrong Size returns ÷ All returns"}]}]},"eda":{"first":true,"label":"The Data","title":"The Data","summary":"The competition provided a simulated dataset of a Vietnamese fashion e-commerce business from 04/07/2012 to 31/12/2022. It contains 15 CSV files split into four layers: Master (reference data), Transaction, Analytical, and Operational. Tables link through order, customer, product, and zip-code keys: each order has exactly one payment, at most one shipment, and zero or more returns and reviews, and inventory has one row per product per month.","facts":[{"label":"Master · reference data","value":"products (2,412 products with price and COGS), customers (~122K, with sign-up date, age group, and acquisition channel), promotions (49 campaigns), geography (~40K zip codes by city and region)"},{"label":"Transaction","value":"orders (~647K), order_items (~715K lines with discounts and promo codes), payments (1:1 with orders), shipments (~566K), returns (~40K with reasons), reviews (~114K ratings)"},{"label":"Analytical","value":"sales: daily revenue and COGS for all 3,833 days, plus a sample submission file for the forecasting part of the competition"},{"label":"Operational","value":"inventory (month-end snapshots with stock on hand, days of supply, and stockout days), an enhanced inventory file with derived metrics, and web_traffic (daily sessions, visitors, bounce rate, and traffic source)"}]},"implementation":{"label":"Part 3 · Segmentation","title":"Who Brings the Revenue","summary":"K-Means ran on the first two principal components of log-scaled RFM. PC1 (77.1% of variance) measures value and engagement: more orders, more spend, and a more recent last order move together. PC2 (17.4%) separates recent buyers from those who stopped long ago. Buyers form one continuous cloud rather than separate islands, so K-Means cuts it into slices along these two axes. That is why the clusters look like wedges and why silhouette is only moderate (0.37): the segments are action groups along a spectrum, not naturally separate types.","visuals":[{"image":"/images/projects/vin/vin-segments-pca.png","width":2000,"height":1280,"alt":"Scatter plot of 15,000 buyers on the first two principal components, colored by five K-Means clusters: Churned on the left, Dormant and Loyal along the top, New / promising at the bottom, and Champions on the right","caption":"Clusters on the PCA plane: Champions sit on the right (most orders and spend, bought recently), Churned on the far left (one order, a median of 6.5 years ago), Dormant and Loyal along the top (stopped buying a while ago), and New / promising at the bottom (bought recently but only a few times)."},{"image":"/images/projects/vin/vin-k-selection.png","width":2600,"height":780,"alt":"Four small charts of silhouette, Davies-Bouldin, stability ARI, and smallest cluster size for k from 2 to 8, with k = 5 highlighted","caption":"Choosing k: k = 2 is too coarse, and k = 3 scores best but merges recent low-value buyers with long-lost ones. k = 4 scores worst (silhouette 0.346, Davies-Bouldin 0.99). k = 5 ties k = 3 on the combined statistical rank, stays stable (ARI 0.956), keeps every cluster above 10% of buyers, and splits out two groups that need different actions: New / promising (10.2%) and Churned (25.5% of buyers, 2.6% of revenue). From k = 6 a cluster drops below 5% and two clusters would get the same action."},{"image":"/images/projects/vin/vin-segments.png","width":2000,"height":1120,"alt":"Bar chart comparing each segment's share of customers and share of revenue: Champions 10.2% vs 43.6%, Loyal 15.7% vs 34.9%, Dormant 20.5% vs 13.1%, New 7.3% vs 5.7%, Churned 18.4% vs 2.6%, Ordered but never converted 2.0% vs 0%, Never ordered 26.0% vs 0%","caption":"Segments: Champions and Loyal customers are 26% of the base but 78.5% of revenue, while 28% of registered customers never completed an order."}],"facts":[{"label":"Champions · 12,384 · 43.6% of revenue","value":"Bought most recently (~5 months), most often (17 orders), and spend the most. Almost only Streetwear (96%), pay by credit card (96%), arrive through organic search, mostly on mobile, and cluster in the West. They rarely come through email (lift 0.49), so the business has no direct line to its best customers."},{"label":"Loyal · 19,103 · 34.9% of revenue","value":"Bought often (9 orders) and spent well, but have not ordered for almost 2 years. Same profile as Champions: Streetwear and credit card, rarely cash on delivery. Yesterday's Champions, drifting toward Dormant."},{"label":"Dormant · 24,995 · 13.1% of revenue","value":"3 orders with above-average order value, but none for ~4.5 years. They order directly or through email (lift 1.53 and 1.35) and use Apple Pay, bank transfer, or cash on delivery, so low-cost email can reach them."},{"label":"New / promising · 8,945 · 5.7% of revenue","value":"Bought recently (~7 months) but only 4 orders so far. They order directly or through email and pay with Apple Pay or cash on delivery. This is when they become Loyal or drop off."},{"label":"Churned · 22,412 · 2.6% of revenue","value":"One low-value order ~6.5 years ago and the highest return rate (7.8%). Unusually heavy on GenZ and Outdoor (lift 2.93 and 2.31), tablets, and PayPal, which suggests (to be tested) that the experience in those categories drove them away."},{"label":"Never ordered · 31,684 · Rule-based","value":"Signed up but never placed an order: 1 in 4 registered customers, slightly more in the Central region. The clearest opportunity at the top of the funnel."},{"label":"Ordered but never converted · 2,407 · Rule-based","value":"Every order was cancelled or abandoned. They meant to buy but hit a barrier; the 152 who failed twice or more show the clearest intent."}]},"results":{"outcomesTitle":"Key Insights","outcomes":["The decline is a conversion and retention problem, not a traffic problem: sessions grew 63% from 2013 to 2022 while conversion (revenue orders ÷ sessions) fell from 1.01% to 0.29%, and 28% of registered customers (34,091) never completed an order.","Promotions are eroding margin without lifting order value: they appear on 38% of orders, AOV tends to drop when they are used, and margin fell from 22% to 14%.","Profit depends on one category: Streetwear drives ~80% of profit. Email brings the highest AOV but low volume, while organic search and social bring volume at lower AOV.","Capital is locked in stock: ~826 days of supply and an inventory-to-sales ratio of 1.15–1.18 mean replenishment runs well ahead of demand.","Wrong size is a leading return reason, adding reverse-logistics and handling cost on top of ~6-day average delivery.","Revenue rests on a small, loyal core: Champions (14% of buyers) bring 43.6% of revenue and Loyal customers (22%) another 34.9%, while Dormant and Churned customers, 54% of buyers whose last order was a median of 4.5–6.5 years ago, bring only 15.7%.","Who customers are matters less than how they buy: age, gender, and acquisition channel barely differ between segments (Cramér's V below 0.01), while favorite category, payment method, and device do (V 0.14–0.25), so targeting should use behavior, not demographics."],"plan":{"title":"Overall recommendation after the models","summary":"The forecast shows revenue flat at about 1.18B VND in 2023 if nothing changes, and promotions explain only 2.6% of it. Segmentation shows where the money is: Champions and Loyal customers are 26% of the base but 78.5% of revenue. So growth has to come from conversion and retention, timed to the seasons the forecast predicts, not from more traffic or deeper discounts. In order of priority:","items":[{"when":"Jan – Mar 2023","action":"Turn sign-ups into first orders","detail":"A time-limited first-order voucher for the 31,684 customers who never ordered. For the 2,407 whose orders were all cancelled or abandoned, find the barrier (payment, price, delivery) and retarget them with the exact products they ordered.","basis":"Dashboard: conversion fell from 1.01% to 0.29%. Segmentation: 28% of registered customers never completed an order.","track":"First-order conversion, conversion rate"},{"when":"From March 2023","action":"Get ready for the April – June peak","detail":"Plan stock, staff, and delivery capacity for the second quarter, which carries the year. Reorder each product from the daily forecast so best-sellers do not run out while slow stock keeps piling up.","basis":"Forecast: April to June is the peak every year. Dashboard: 67% stockout and 76% overstock at the same time.","track":"Stockout rate, overstock rate, days of supply"},{"when":"Ongoing","action":"Protect the core: Champions and Loyal","detail":"VIP perks and early access without deep discounts for Champions, and tiered points with cross-sell for Loyal customers, who have not ordered for almost 2 years. Build an email relationship with Champions, who rarely come through email today.","basis":"Segmentation: Champions bring 43.6% and Loyal 34.9% of revenue; Champions' email lift is only 0.49.","track":"Retention and AOV of Champions, order frequency of Loyal"},{"when":"Before July 2023","action":"Cut the depth of the odd-year Urban Blowout","detail":"Replace blanket discounts with targeted ones: vouchers only above the current AOV, a discount floor that keeps margin at 10% or more, deep offers only for at-risk and new customers, and a 10% holdout group to measure real promotion ROI.","basis":"Forecast: Q3 2023 sells at a loss (COGS 1.05× revenue) when the 50% campaign runs. Dashboard: promoted orders have a lower AOV.","track":"Q3 gross margin, promotion ROI against the holdout"},{"when":"Every month","action":"Time campaigns and replenishment to month-end","detail":"Schedule campaigns, stock top-ups, and delivery capacity for the last days of each month instead of spreading them evenly.","basis":"Forecast: the last 3 days of a month are forecast at 5.55M VND a day, nearly double mid-month (2.92M).","track":"Month-end revenue, stockouts in the last week"},{"when":"Q2 – Q3 2023","action":"Fix the reasons customers left before winning them back","detail":"Add size guides, measurements, and fit labels, starting with the top 20 return SKUs, then run low-cost seasonal win-back email for Dormant customers and only near-zero-cost email for Churned ones.","basis":"Dashboard: wrong size is about 35% of returns in every category. Segmentation: Churned customers have the highest return rate (7.8%) and lean on GenZ and Outdoor.","track":"Wrong-size share, reactivation rate, cost per recovered order"},{"when":"2024","action":"Reduce reliance on Streetwear","detail":"Pilot new Outdoor SKUs, cross-sell Outdoor and Casual to Streetwear buyers, and move part of the paid budget to email, the highest-AOV channel.","basis":"Dashboard: Streetwear brings 1.05bn of the 1.36bn gross profit.","track":"Gross profit share outside Streetwear"}]}}}},{"id":11,"slug":"corporate-loan-portfolio-analytics","theme":"Data Analytics","title":"Corporate Loan Portfolio Analytics","category":"Banking Analytics · Corporate Lending","description":"A bank grew its corporate loan book 10.9% in a year to VND 98.4 trillion, yet half-year net interest income rose only 2.8% and bad debt climbed from 2.12% to 2.63%. Using a simulated dataset of 10,000 business customers and 31,000 loans, this Power BI analysis traces where the growth comes from, why margins are thinning, and where risk is building up.","tags":["Power BI","DAX","Power Query","Data Modeling","Python","pandas","Banking Analytics","Credit Risk"],"link":"#","github":"https://github.com/AIVIETNAM-AIO-thuhuynhtran05/Corporate-Loan-Portfolio-Analytics","powerBiProject":"https://github.com/AIVIETNAM-AIO-thuhuynhtran05/Corporate-Loan-Portfolio-Analytics/tree/main/dashboard","powerBiDownload":"/downloads/aiv-bank-cltd-dashboard-pbip.zip","liveDashboard":"/demos/aiv-bank-loan-dashboard.html","image":"/images/projects/corporate-loan-portfolio.svg","facts":[{"value":"10,000","label":"Corporate customers"},{"value":"31,104","label":"Loan contracts"},{"value":"VND 98.4T","label":"Loans at 30/06/2026"},{"value":"2.63%","label":"Bad debt (OD3–5)"}],"details":{"challenge":{"label":"The Problem","title":"Growth That Does Not Pay Off","summary":"AIV Bank is a simulated Vietnamese bank used in the eDA program (Module 2) at AI VIET NAM. Its corporate banking division (KHDN) needs a monthly report that shows the loan book, the income it earns, and its credit quality on one screen, with every number compared against last month and the same month last year. At 30/06/2026 the headline numbers pull in different directions.","points":["Loans grew 10.9% year on year to VND 98,367 billion, and the average balance YTD grew 13.3%, but net interest income (NII) YTD grew only 2.8%.","Bad debt (OD3–5) held at 2.1–2.2% through 2025, then rose four months in a row to 2.63%; OD3–5 balances are up 37.8% year on year.","Managers need to know which segments, industries, and business units drive each of these moves, not just the bank-wide totals."]},"solution":{"label":"Approach","title":"From EDA to a Monthly Report","summary":"A descriptive EDA in Python found the patterns and the business rules; the Power BI report then turns them into a repeatable monthly view.","steps":["Profiled the 14-table star schema (about 723K rows) in pandas: no duplicate rows or broken foreign keys, and every missing value has a business reason (open contracts have no close date, unsecured loans have no LTV).","Described customers, contracts, and month-end balances, always putting the share of customers next to the share of loans, since the two differ sharply; measured concentration with top-N shares and a Gini coefficient.","Traced trends over 30 month-ends (12/2023 – 06/2026) for loans, NII, fee income, TOI, and debt group ratios, plus debt group migration and internal vs credit bureau (CIC) classification.","Loaded the workbook into Power BI with Power Query and wrote 154 DAX measures that follow banking rules: balances are read at one month-end, income is summed over the period.","Designed four report pages with shared filters for reporting month, segment, and product, and built an interactive HTML version of the same pages that opens in any browser."]},"architecture":{"title":"Report Flow","flow":["Excel star schema (14 tables)","pandas EDA","Power Query load","DAX measures (LM / LY)","4-page Power BI report","Interactive HTML version"],"beforeTitle":"Before","before":["Loan, income, and debt group data sat in separate fact tables, at different grains (month-end snapshot vs monthly flow).","Totals hid the shift toward large clients and the pockets of rising bad debt in a few industries and branches."],"afterTitle":"After","after":["One header filter drives every page, and every KPI card shows the change against last month-end and the same month last year.","Managers can go from the bank-wide bad debt ratio down to the industry and business unit behind it in two clicks."]},"kpis":{"label":"Dashboard","title":"KPIs & Pages","summary":"Four pages, one question each. All amounts are in billion VND. Balances are a stock, so they are read at the selected month-end and never added across months; NII, NFI, and TOI are flows, so they are summed from January to the selected month.","root":{"title":"Power BI Report","subtitle":"4 pages · 154 DAX measures"},"groups":[{"title":"Overview","question":"How is the portfolio doing overall?","items":[{"name":"Outstanding loans","formula":"Balance at the selected month-end"},{"name":"DNBQ YTD","full":"Average balance YTD","formula":"Sum of monthly average balances ÷ Months elapsed"},{"name":"TOI YTD","full":"Total operating income","formula":"NII YTD + NFI YTD"}]},{"title":"Loan balance","question":"Where does the loan book sit?","items":[{"name":"Term share","formula":"Balance of the term ÷ Total balance"},{"name":"Change vs last month","formula":"(Balance − Balance LM) ÷ Balance LM"},{"name":"Top 10 business units","formula":"Ranked by balance at month-end"}]},{"title":"NII/TOI","question":"Who generates the income?","items":[{"name":"NII YTD","full":"Net interest income","formula":"Interest income − Funding cost (FTP), Jan to month"},{"name":"Change vs same period","formula":"(NII YTD − NII YTD LY) ÷ NII YTD LY"},{"name":"Top 10 customers","formula":"Ranked by NII YTD and TOI YTD"}]},{"title":"Credit quality","question":"Where is risk building up?","items":[{"name":"OD2 ratio","full":"Special-mention loans","formula":"Debt group 2 balance ÷ Total balance"},{"name":"OD3–5 ratio","full":"Bad debt","formula":"Debt groups 3–5 balance ÷ Total balance"},{"name":"Change in points","formula":"Ratio − Ratio LM"}]}],"modelTitle":"Data model","visuals":[{"image":"/images/projects/aiv-bank/aiv-data-model.png","width":1536,"height":1234,"alt":"Power BI data model: dim_loan_contract in the center links to dim_customer, dim_product, dim_campaign, and dim_branch; dim_customer links to dim_industry, dim_segment, and fact_debt_group_cic; fact_loan_balance, fact_income_monthly, and fact_due_soon link to the contract, with dim_date, dim_debt_group, and a fact_plan table linked to dim_branch","caption":"Data model: 9 dimensions and 5 fact tables. Every loan fact links to dim_loan_contract, which leads to the customer, product, campaign, and branch; customers link on to industry, segment, and their CIC debt group, and the plan table joins at branch level."}],"dashboard":{"title":"The dashboard","summary":"Every page shares the same header: reporting month, segment, and product filters, a reset button, and the report date. Open the interactive version from the button at the top of this page.","visuals":[{"image":"/images/projects/aiv-bank/aiv-dashboard-overview.png","width":1800,"height":1008,"alt":"Overview page: six KPI cards for outstanding loans, average balance YTD, NII YTD, TOI YTD, OD2 ratio, and OD3–5 ratio, with monthly trend lines for loans, NII and TOI, and debt group ratios from 01/2025 to 06/2026","caption":"Overview: loans climb steadily to 98,367 bn, while monthly NII stays flat at about 132 bn and the OD3–5 ratio turns up from 1.99% in January to 2.63% in June 2026."},{"image":"/images/projects/aiv-bank/aiv-dashboard-loan-balance.png","width":1800,"height":1588,"alt":"Loan balance page: balance by term, a pie chart by customer segment, a treemap by industry, a bar chart by product, and a table of the top 10 business units with month-on-month and year-on-year changes","caption":"Loan balance: short-term loans are 63.9% of the book; BIGCORP, UPPER 1, and UPPER 2 together hold 44.1%; construction (12,946 bn) and wholesale trade (10,185 bn) are the largest industries."},{"image":"/images/projects/aiv-bank/aiv-dashboard-nii-toi.png","width":1800,"height":1906,"alt":"NII/TOI page: NII YTD and TOI YTD cards, pie charts by segment, YTD trend lines, top 10 customers by NII and TOI, and treemaps of the top 10 business units","caption":"NII/TOI: MICRO firms earn 32.6% of NII YTD while holding 20.2% of loans, since small loans carry higher rates; the single largest customer alone brings 16.15 bn of NII."},{"image":"/images/projects/aiv-bank/aiv-dashboard-credit-quality.png","width":1800,"height":1499,"alt":"Credit quality page: OD2 and OD3–5 ratio and balance cards, debt group mix for the top 10 industries, a table of OD ratios for the top 10 business units, and monthly OD2 and OD3–5 trends","caption":"Credit quality: bad debt reaches 9.2% in electrical manufacturing and 6.2% in real estate, and Trung tâm KHDN Bắc 1 jumps from 2.37% to 8.94% OD3–5 in a single month."}]}},"implementation":{"title":"Technical Implementation","facts":[{"label":"Dataset","value":"14 tables · 10,000 customers · 31,104 contracts · 78 business units · 30 month-ends (12/2023 – 06/2026)"},{"label":"Model","value":"Star schema: 9 dimensions, 5 facts (month-end balance, monthly income, plan, principal due soon, CIC debt group)"},{"label":"DAX","value":"154 measures in numbered business folders: stock vs flow logic, last-month (LM) and last-year (LY) comparisons, dynamic Top 10 ranking, conditional colors"},{"label":"Report","value":"4 Power BI pages saved as PBIP (PBIR + TMDL), plus a self-contained interactive HTML version"},{"label":"EDA","value":"Python (pandas, matplotlib): 62 summary tables and 33 charts"},{"label":"Context","value":"eDA program, Module 2 · AI VIET NAM (2026) · synthetic data"}]},"results":{"outcomesTitle":"Key Insights","validationTitle":"Recommendations","outcomes":["Margins are thinning: loans grew 10.9% but NII only 2.8%, so the net interest margin slipped from about 1.9% to 1.7%. Growth is coming from large clients, who borrow more at lower rates.","The book is moving up-market: MICRO and SMALL firms are 83.5% of borrowers but now hold 34.4% of loans (40.8% in 12/2023), while UPPER and BIGCORP clients, 6.1% of borrowers, hold 44.1%. The top 10% of borrowers hold 68% of the book (Gini 0.77).","Bad debt has turned: after a flat 2025, the OD3–5 ratio rose four months in a row, and loans that fall past OD2 rarely recover (only about 5% of OD3 loans return to normal).","Risk is concentrated: bad debt is 9.2% in electrical manufacturing, 6.2% in real estate, and 4.1% on medium and long-term loans, against 2.6% overall.","Early warnings sit outside the bank: of 573 customers that the credit bureau (CIC) rates as debt group 2 or worse, 124 (21.6%) are still rated better internally."],"validation":["Price for margin, not only volume: review rates on new large-client loans against funding cost; track NII growth vs average balance growth.","Watch the OD2 pool closely: 15% of OD2 loans fell to OD3 in June, so set up monthly reviews for OD2 borrowers; track the OD2 → OD3 roll rate.","Tighten limits where bad debt is high: real estate, electrical manufacturing, and medium and long-term loans; track the OD3–5 ratio by industry and term.","Align internal ratings with CIC: reclassify the 124 customers rated worse at CIC and check them before renewing loans; track the internal vs CIC mismatch rate.","Follow up on Trung tâm KHDN Bắc 1, where bad debt jumped 6.57 points in one month; track OD3–5 by business unit each month."]}}},{"id":14,"slug":"itb-customer-health-engagement-scoring","theme":"Data Analytics","title":"ITB Round 2 – Customer Financial Health & Engagement Scoring","category":"Credit Scoring · Customer Analytics","description":"A consumer-finance lender with 1.0M customers answered two questions in separate teams: can this customer repay, and will they borrow again? In a 30-hour hackathon round, we built two explainable 0–100 scores from contracts, applications, leads, and calls, then combined them into seven segments, so every customer gets one next best action and a plain-language reason.","tags":["Python","pandas","LightGBM","SHAP","WoE Scorecard","Credit Scoring","Customer Segmentation","Next Best Action"],"link":"#","github":"https://github.com/AIVIETNAM-AIO-thuhuynhtran05/ITB-Round-2-Customer-Financial-Health-Engagement-Scoring","presentation":{"pdf":"/slides/itb-round2-health-engagement-slides.pdf","slides":["/slides/itb-r2/slide-01.jpg","/slides/itb-r2/slide-02.jpg","/slides/itb-r2/slide-03.jpg","/slides/itb-r2/slide-04.jpg","/slides/itb-r2/slide-05.jpg","/slides/itb-r2/slide-06.jpg","/slides/itb-r2/slide-07.jpg","/slides/itb-r2/slide-08.jpg","/slides/itb-r2/slide-09.jpg","/slides/itb-r2/slide-10.jpg","/slides/itb-r2/slide-11.jpg","/slides/itb-r2/slide-12.jpg","/slides/itb-r2/slide-13.jpg","/slides/itb-r2/slide-14.jpg","/slides/itb-r2/slide-15.jpg","/slides/itb-r2/slide-16.jpg"]},"image":"/images/projects/itb-round2-scoring.svg","related":{"label":"Round 3 of the competition (final)","title":"Shop Growth Potential","text":"The final round moved from customers to the shops that bring them in: how far each of 11,795 partner shops can realistically grow, what holds it back, and where Sales Management should invest. The scores from this round, set in June 2025, still ranked customers correctly on 16 months of new data there. The team finished runner-up of 200 teams.","href":"/projects/itb-shop-growth-potential","cta":"See Round 3"},"facts":[{"value":"1,000,528","label":"Customers scored"},{"value":"2.89M","label":"Disbursed contracts"},{"value":"0.674 / 0.702","label":"AUC on unseen data (health / engagement)"},{"value":"7","label":"Segments, each with a next best action"}],"details":{"challenge":{"label":"The Brief","title":"Two Scores, One Action per Customer","summary":"Round 2 of the ITB Competition (Business Intelligence Season 10, UEL) gave teams 30 hours to turn raw lending data into customer intelligence. The lender has customer profiles, past contracts and applications, leads, and call logs, but no single view of how financially healthy each customer is, how engaged they are, or what to do next with each one. The brief required explainable scores, warned against reading correlation as causation, and ruled out judging risk by demographics without evidence.","points":["<strong>Challenge 1 – Financial Health Score:</strong> which factors reflect financial health, a 0–100 score, customer groups, positive and negative drivers, and evidence that the method is sound.","<strong>Challenge 2 – Engagement Score:</strong> recency, frequency, interaction, lead activity, and product interaction, turned into a score and segments, with drivers and proof that the score tracks business outcomes.","<strong>Challenge 3 – Segmentation & Next Best Action:</strong> combine both scores, find the opportunities, and recommend one action per customer with the reason behind it."]},"solution":{"summary":"One shared data base, then one model per challenge. Each challenge has its own phase below that shows how its score or segments are built.","steps":["Cleaned five raw tables into one leakage-free customer base: removed 1.95M contract rows duplicated by a profile join and 39K duplicate leads, turned placeholders (XNA, 0, the 3000-01-01 sentinel date) and implausible values (age 2–124, 99 children) into flagged missing values, and unified product codes, provinces, and 248 raw call outcomes.","Fixed the scoring date at 04/06/2025 and built every input only from data known before its cut-off. Calls after the scoring date were kept aside to check the scores against real outcomes.","Challenge 1: a Financial Health score from repayment and credit behaviour (LightGBM with monotonic constraints, explained with SHAP), cut into bands A–E plus N for thin files.","Challenge 2: an Engagement score from borrowing and application behaviour (two WoE scorecards on one 0–100 scale), cut into Champions, Active, Warm, and Dormant.","Challenge 3: a rule-based matrix of the two scores that reuses their cut-offs, gives seven segments, and assigns one next best action per segment, with risk blocked first."]},"architecture":{"flow":["5 raw tables · 1.0M customers","Cleaning & leakage-free snapshots","Challenge 1: Financial Health (LightGBM + SHAP)","Challenge 2: Engagement (WoE scorecards)","Challenge 3: Health × Engagement matrix","Next best action with a reason"],"before":["Credit and sales teams each saw part of a customer's history, so \"can they repay?\" and \"will they borrow again?\" were answered separately.","Sales effort was spread evenly: a healthy customer could sit dormant while an engaged but stretched customer kept getting offers."],"after":["Every customer has two explainable 0–100 scores, a segment, one next best action, and the reasons behind it.","Risk is checked before any sales action, and offers go first to the healthy, active customers who bring most of the value."]},"eda":{"summary":"Before scoring, we checked data quality across all five tables and described who the customers are, how they borrow, and how the sales team reaches them.","facts":[{"label":"Data","value":"1,000,528 customers; 2.89M disbursed contracts worth about VND 30.8 trillion, 849K still active; plus applications, leads, past app and web behaviour, and call logs up to 08/2026."},{"label":"One seasonal cohort","value":"Every customer applied during Tết (Dec 2024 – Jan 2025), when applications jumped to 570–660K a month, about 12× the normal rate. Customers hold 4.3 contracts on average."},{"label":"Products","value":"Consumer durable loans are 73% of contracts (median VND 5.3M); cash loans are only 13% but match them in value (median VND 21M). One in three applications is rejected or cancelled."},{"label":"Uneven data","value":"18.2% of customers never had a loan disbursed, 64.5% have a lead, 36% have ever been called, and only 7% used the app in 2025, so customers without loans need their own scoring path."}],"visuals":[{"image":"/slides/itb-r2/slide-04.jpg","width":1920,"height":1080,"alt":"Data issues by table: duplicated contracts and leads, placeholder values such as XNA and 3000-01-01, implausible ages, mixed channel codes, and calls after the snapshot date, with how each was handled","caption":"Data issues found in each table and how they were handled: duplicates removed, placeholders flagged as missing, and a fixed scoring date so no later data leaks into the scores."},{"image":"/slides/itb-r2/slide-06.jpg","width":1920,"height":1080,"alt":"Four panels: overdue rate by product and contract status, share of customers by data available, lead volume versus deals by lead source, and outcomes of connected calls including collection calls","caption":"Risk is low on average (about 2% of contracts are late) but concentrated: 6.2% of customers have been late at least once. Collection calls are 4.4% of calls, so they count toward financial health, not engagement."}]},"models":[{"label":"Challenge 1","title":"Financial Health Score","question":"Can this customer keep up with repayments?","summary":"A gradient-boosting model learns which behaviours come before a missed payment. Its output is rescaled to 0–100 like a credit scorecard, so every point has the same meaning, and SHAP splits each score into the reasons behind it.","steps":["<strong>Define \"bad\".</strong> A contract signed in the 2 months after a cut-off date that is late in its first 4 instalments or written off. A contract needs 125 days before it counts, so its first instalments have had time to fall due.","<strong>Take snapshots.</strong> One row per customer at five monthly cut-offs (Feb–Oct 2024), each built only from data known before that date, over windows of 3, 6, and 12 months and 3 years.","<strong>Build indicators.</strong> 55 candidates in seven groups: repayment history, lender trust and pressure, financial buffer, debt burden, income and stability, income left after instalments, and loan insurance. Demographics, contract status, and call data are left out by design.","<strong>Select.</strong> Drop near-duplicate indicators (correlation above 0.99) and any indicator whose SHAP share is below 0.5%, which leaves 37.","<strong>Train.</strong> LightGBM with monotonic constraints, so that, for example, more late payments can never raise the score. Trained on the first four cut-offs, tuned on October 2024, and tested on December 2024, a period the model never saw.","<strong>Scale to 0–100.</strong> Score = 50 + 15 ÷ ln 2 × (log-odds of good − average log-odds of good in training). 50 is an average customer, and every +15 points doubles the odds of repaying. Because SHAP values add up on the same log-odds scale, each indicator group adds or removes a stated number of points.","<strong>Cut into bands.</strong> Fixed score cut-offs give bands A–E. A customer with no loan past 3 instalments has too little history to judge and goes to band N, whatever the score says."],"tables":[{"caption":"Bands: late rate on the December 2024 test period and share of customers at 05/06/2025","columns":["Band","Score","Late rate","Share of customers"],"rows":[["A · Very healthy","65 and above","1.0%","32.4%"],["B · Healthy","58–65","1.6%","10.6%"],["C · Average","50–58","2.0%","11.9%"],["D · Weak","40–50","2.7%","10.8%"],["E · High risk","below 40","6.1%","15.7%"],["N · Thin file","no loan past 3 instalments","4.5%","18.7%"]]}],"visuals":[{"image":"/slides/itb-r2/slide-10.jpg","width":1920,"height":1080,"alt":"Financial Health bands A to E and N with late rates rising from about 1% to 6%, SHAP drivers of the score, and the score distribution of all customers","caption":"Each customer gets a score, a band, and the reasons behind it; the lender's own view and repayment history drive about 70% of the score. The slide is from the competition deck, built on an earlier model run, so its band shares differ slightly from the table above."}],"facts":[{"label":"How well it ranks","value":"AUC 0.769 in training and 0.674 on the unseen December 2024 period, against 0.661 for a traditional WoE scorecard. The late rate rises in order from band A to band E."},{"label":"What drives it","value":"Removing the lender-trust group costs 0.018 AUC and the repayment-history group 0.016; removing income, debt, or balance changes almost nothing. A late payment in the last 12 months raises risk 11.9×."},{"label":"How stable the bands are","value":"Moving every band cut-off by ±2 points moves about 10% of customers to a neighbouring band, and changing the 15-point scaling to 10 or 20 moves 10–18%. In every case the late rate still rises in order from A to E."}]},{"label":"Challenge 2","title":"Engagement Score","question":"Will this customer borrow again soon?","summary":"A points-based scorecard: each behaviour is grouped into readable ranges, each range earns points from how often customers in it came back, and the points add up to 0–100. Customers who have never had a loan get their own scorecard, because every loan-based behaviour is zero for them.","steps":["<strong>Define the outcome.</strong> The customer files an application that is signed, or a lead turns into a deal, within 60 days after a cut-off date.","<strong>Learn on two dates, test on a third.</strong> Learn on 01/02 and 01/03/2025 together, using a random half of the customers. Test on the other half and again on 01/04/2025, a date never used for learning.","<strong>Turn factors into candidates.</strong> Behaviours mapped to the brief's five aspects (recency, frequency, interaction, lead activity, product interaction) plus the application journey. App sessions are excluded because they are measured after the cut-off, and collection calls belong to financial health.","<strong>Group and smooth.</strong> Each behaviour is split into readable ranges, and an isotonic fit makes sure more activity never lowers the points. This removes the Tết effect: any loan in the last 2 years earns the same points, and only customers silent for longer lose them.","<strong>Select.</strong> Keep a behaviour only if its information value is at least 0.02, its direction makes business sense, it is stable over time (PSI ≤ 0.25), it does not repeat another behaviour (WoE correlation ≤ 0.7), and its weight stays positive next to the others. 8 of 26 candidates survive for customers with loans, 7 of 20 for thin files.","<strong>Put both scorecards on one scale.</strong> Score = A + B × predicted log-odds, with A and B set so the 1st percentile is 0 and the 99th is 100. A score of 60 means the same chance of coming back in either scorecard.","<strong>Cut into segments.</strong> Cut-offs sit where the conversion rate reaches 0.5×, 1×, and 2× the average: Champions ≥ 80.1, Active ≥ 56.8, Warm ≥ 37.0, Dormant below."],"tables":[{"caption":"Share of points each aspect can add","columns":["Aspect","Customers with loans","Thin-file customers"],"rows":[["Frequency (loans signed, active months, scoring checks)","49%","0%"],["Application journey (gap between applications)","19%","24%"],["Recency (time since last loan or eligibility check)","18%","41%"],["Product interaction (repeat borrowing)","8%","0%"],["Lead activity (lead acceptance and response)","5%","29%"],["Interaction behaviour (self-service rate)","0%","6%"]]},{"caption":"Segments: share at 04/06/2025 and 60-day conversion on the out-of-time test","columns":["Segment","Score","Share","Conversion","Profile"],"rows":[["Champions","80.1 and above","17%","14.4%","4.5 active months a year, 10.6 eligibility checks"],["Active","56.8–80.1","27%","8.8%","2.0 active months, 2.5 checks"],["Warm","37.0–56.8","26%","4.8%","Mostly a single Tết application"],["Dormant","below 37.0","31%","2.3%","58% never had a loan; 74% have no running loan"]]}],"visuals":[{"image":"/slides/itb-r2/slide-12.jpg","width":1920,"height":1080,"alt":"Engagement score validation on held-out customers: conversion by decile, a gain curve, a re-test at a later date, and AUC compared with RFM, equal weights, and gradient boosting","caption":"Validation as presented in the competition deck. The repository notebook holds the final run, whose numbers are quoted on this page."}],"facts":[{"label":"How well it ranks","value":"AUC 0.715 on held-out customers and 0.702 on the out-of-time date (KS 0.304). The top decile converts at 15.7% against 1.3% for the bottom, and the top 30% of customers hold 56% of converters."},{"label":"Against alternatives","value":"Simple RFM ranking reaches 0.635. A monotone LightGBM reaches 0.710, only 0.008 more, while relying on a behaviour the scorecard dropped as unstable, so the scorecard was kept."},{"label":"Linked to business outcomes","value":"Champions and Active are 41% of customers but 81% of new booked value. After the scoring date, a connected sales call ends in interest 6.1% of the time for Champions against 2.7% for Dormant, while the answer rate is flat at 18–21%."}]},{"label":"Challenge 3","title":"From Two Scores to Seven Segments","question":"What should we do with each customer, and why?","summary":"The two scores answer different questions and overlap only partly (Spearman ρ = 0.49): 35% of band A customers are barely engaged, and 43% of band E customers are highly engaged. So we crossed them in a simple matrix instead of a clustering model. It reuses the cut-offs from Challenges 1 and 2, adds no new parameters, and uses no demographics.","steps":["<strong>Collapse Financial Health into three tiers.</strong> High = bands A and B (score 58 and above), Mid = C and D (40–58), Low = E (below 40). Band N becomes its own group, Insufficient History.","<strong>Collapse Engagement into two tiers.</strong> High = Champions and Active (56.8 and above), Low = Warm and Dormant.","<strong>Cross them.</strong> 3 × 2 = six segments, plus Insufficient History, gives seven.","<strong>Apply rules in priority order.</strong> Block risk first (no new credit for low health or recent lateness), then apply the segment action, then adjust product, channel, and timing to each customer's history, such as offering a renewal when a loan is about to end.","<strong>Explain every action.</strong> Each customer gets one action and a plain-language reason built from the drivers of both scores."],"tables":[{"caption":"Segment matrix and next best action","columns":["Segment","Rule","Share","Next best action"],"rows":[["Stars","High health × high engagement","27%","Upsell and cross-sell; renewal offer when a loan ends"],["Sleeping Gems","High health × low engagement","16%","Time-limited pre-approved offer through app push or SMS"],["Growth","Mid health × high engagement","10%","Small limit with a down payment, through telesales and the app"],["Cool Prospects","Mid health × low engagement","13%","Low-cost nurture, no credit push until a need appears"],["Stressed Seekers","Low health × high engagement","7%","No new credit; counselling and repayment reminders"],["At-Risk Dormant","Low health × low engagement","9%","Early-warning monitoring, no marketing"],["Insufficient History","Band N","19%","Starter BNPL limit that grows with each on-time instalment"]]}],"visuals":[{"image":"/slides/itb-r2/slide-15.jpg","width":1920,"height":1080,"alt":"Table of seven segments with profile, next best action, and business objective, from Stars to Insufficient History","caption":"Each segment gets one next best action and a business objective, with an A/B test and a 10% control group before rollout."}]}],"implementation":{"label":"Segments","title":"What Each Segment Is Worth","summary":"Expected new lending = the segment's 60-day conversion rate × the customer's expected loan size, counted only for customers who receive an offer. Risk-blocked customers and segments with no credit offer count as zero.","facts":[{"label":"Stars · 266,519 customers","value":"1.2% ever overdue, 11.1% expected conversion. About VND 452 bn of expected new lending, 73% of the total, mainly BNPL (39%) and cash loans (37%)."},{"label":"Sleeping Gems · 163,809","value":"The healthiest group (0.3% ever overdue) but only 4.2% expected conversion. About VND 115 bn, reached cheaply through the app."},{"label":"Growth · 97,332","value":"3.4% ever overdue with a clear credit need. About VND 48 bn, mostly small BNPL limits (82%)."},{"label":"Stressed Seekers & At-Risk Dormant · 156,672","value":"About 27% ever overdue. No credit offer; 17–21% of them are blocked by risk rules outright."},{"label":"Insufficient History · 186,862","value":"Almost no repayment track record and the youngest group. Small starter limits build the history that later scoring needs."},{"label":"Rollout test","value":"A/B test with a 10% control group per segment; the safety metric is the delinquency rate, and engagement cut-offs are recalibrated every quarter."}]},"results":{"outcomesTitle":"Key Insights","validationTitle":"Recommendations","outcomes":["Repayment history and lender trust decide financial health, not income: a late payment in the last 12 months raises risk 11.9×, while income and debt together barely move the model.","Lateness repeats and fades slowly: one earlier late contract lifts the next contract's late rate from 1.4% to 10.2%; a late payment within 6 months means 36.9%, and it is still 4.3% after 4 years.","Engagement follows the loan cycle: a customer who has just finished a loan is 2.07× as likely to borrow again, while having no running loan halves the odds (×0.53). More leads or calls from the company do not raise engagement.","Value is concentrated: Stars are 27% of customers but 73% of expected new lending.","The two scores add different information: many of the healthiest customers are barely engaged and many of the riskiest are highly engaged, which is why they need different actions."],"validation":["Block risk first: no new credit for low-health customers; counselling and repayment reminders for Stressed Seekers, and monitoring only for At-Risk Dormant.","Focus sales calls on Stars and Growth, and time offers to when a loan is ending instead of adding more leads.","Reach Sleeping Gems through app push and SMS with a pre-approved offer, at minimal cost.","Give thin-file customers a starter limit that grows with each on-time instalment, instead of scoring them like everyone else.","Run an A/B test with a 10% control group before wide rollout, and recalibrate the engagement cut-offs every quarter."]}}},{"id":7,"slug":"itb-customer-financial-health","theme":"Data Analytics","title":"ITB Round 1 – Customer Financial Health Analysis","category":"Customer Analytics · Responsible Spending","description":"Home Credit's customers are loyal and active, but their financial health rises and falls with their own spending pace. Using a year of card data, we mapped who is thriving, who is stretched, and who is drifting away, and proposed non-punitive ways to help each group spend within their means.","tags":["SQL","Python","Excel","scikit-learn","K-Means","Customer Segmentation","Consumer Finance Analytics"],"link":"#","github":"https://github.com/AIVIETNAM-AIO-thuhuynhtran05/ITB-Customer-Financial-Health-Analysis","presentation":{"pdf":"/slides/itb-customer-financial-health-slides.pdf","slides":["/slides/itb/slide-01.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-02.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-03.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-04.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-05.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-06.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-07.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-08.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-09.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-10.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-11.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-12.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-13.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-14.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-15.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-16.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-17.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-18.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-19.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-20.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-21.jpg","/slides/itb/slide-22.jpg"]},"image":"/images/projects/itb-financial-health.svg","related":{"label":"Round 2 of the competition","title":"Customer Financial Health & Engagement Scoring","text":"The next round switched to a lender's data on 1.0M customers: a 30-hour hackathon to score each customer's financial health and engagement and give each one a next best action. The final round then assessed how far each partner shop can grow, and the team finished runner-up of 200 teams.","href":"/projects/itb-customer-health-engagement-scoring","cta":"See Round 2"},"facts":[{"value":"999","label":"Customers"},{"value":"1.85M","label":"Card transactions"},{"value":"81%","label":"Health score explained"},{"value":"5","label":"Customer segments"}],"details":{"challenge":{"title":"The Brief & Questions Answered","summary":"Home Credit Vietnam, a consumer-finance company, wants to move from a reactive, collections-oriented view of customers to a proactive financial-wellbeing view. It wants to better understand customers' financial wellbeing, spending behavior, and engagement with its transaction channels: how these patterns vary across customer groups, how financial health and engagement relate to one another, and whether distinct behavioral patterns can be identified across the customer base. The data is a synthetic 2025 dataset that Home Credit provided for the competition, so ratios matter more than absolute VND amounts. The assumed audience is the company's leadership and customer-experience teams, and the health score is a wellbeing indicator, never a credit decision tool.","points":["<strong>How healthy are customers, and how do they spend and use channels?</strong> Most customer-months look Stable (72.5%), yet 87% of customers have at least one month below 60. Spending peaks in December (2.8× February) because customers transact more often, pushing 84% of them below 60 that month. Almost everyone uses all five channels, but POS still carries 59% of transactions and only 21% of spend is online.","<strong>How do these patterns vary across customer groups?</strong> Less than expected: how people spend matters far more than who they are. Spend-to-income explains 81% of the health score, about 4× the effect of job, province, or age; health differs by only 1.2 points across age groups, and gaps between provinces are not statistically significant. Age does shape digital use, with digital share falling steadily from younger to older customers.","<strong>How do financial health and engagement relate?</strong> They pull apart: 106 loyal daily users (16.5% of spend) are the most financially stretched, while 85 financially healthy, mostly older customers barely use the card.","<strong>Can distinct behavioral patterns be identified?</strong> Yes: K-Means on 8 behavioral features (stable across 50 bootstrap samples, ARI 0.97) gives five segments: Healthy Core (299), Emerging Digital (184), Stretched & Engaged (226), Low-Engagement Vulnerable (199), and New / Low-history (91). The boundaries are soft, since customers form a continuum rather than sharply separated types.","<strong>What should the company do?</strong> Six non-punitive interventions, such as budgeting tools, spend alerts, year-end planning reminders, and digital nudges, prioritized by reach and rolled out over 10 weeks to cover 90.9% of customers."]},"solution":{"summary":"Owned the work end to end in SQL, Python, Excel, and scikit-learn: preparing and validating the data, exploring spending patterns, engineering customer features, segmenting customers with K-Means, and designing a customer-care strategy.","steps":["Prepared the data in SQL and checked quality in Excel and Python: missing values, duplicates, and outliers across 1.85M card transactions.","Visualized spending patterns over time: the December peak (2.8× the lowest month) comes from 2.9× more transactions, not bigger baskets.","Analyzed financial-health drivers: spend-to-income alone explains 81% of the health score (ρ = −0.95), and “tough months” jump from near zero below 70% of income to 93% above 87%.","Engineered customer-level features such as spend-to-income ratio, transaction frequency, average ticket size, online spend share, and engagement indicators.","Segmented customers with K-Means on 8 behavioral features (stable across 50 bootstrap samples, ARI 0.97) into Healthy Core, Emerging Digital, Stretched & Engaged, Low-Engagement Vulnerable, and New / Low-history.","Profiled each segment by health, spending pressure, engagement, and digital behavior, flagging 106 customers who are highly engaged but financially stretched.","Designed 6 non-punitive interventions prioritized by reach and driver strength, in a 10-week rollout plan covering 90.9% of customers."]},"architecture":{"flow":["1.85M card transactions · 999 customers","SQL data prep & EDA","Financial-health drivers","K-Means · 5 segments","6 non-punitive interventions"],"before":["Loyalty and activity alone do not explain a customer’s financial-health trajectory.","A single engagement approach may not fit every spending pattern."],"after":["Customers are grouped into five segments based on financial health and engagement patterns.","Recommendations focus on supporting healthy spending rather than increasing it indiscriminately."]},"eda":{"summary":"Before analyzing financial health, I checked data quality and explored how, when, where, and through which channels customers spend, so later findings rest on a clean, well-understood base.","facts":[{"label":"Data","value":"1,852,394 transactions and 10,992 customer-months for 999 customers and 693 merchants, Jan 1 – Dec 31, 2025"},{"label":"Data quality","value":"0 duplicate transaction IDs, 0 duplicate customer-month keys, 0 missing values in transactions; all spend amounts are positive"},{"label":"Coverage","value":"908 full-year customers; 91 customers with only 1–2 observed months are kept in overall distributions but excluded from group comparisons"},{"label":"Definitions","value":"Digital = any non-POS channel; online = E-commerce, Mobile App, and Recurring (QR is flagged offline); health score is a wellbeing indicator, not a credit score"}],"visuals":[{"image":"/images/projects/itb/itb-eda-monthly-spend.png","width":1820,"height":1127,"alt":"Total spend by month in 2025, with December at 488 billion VND and February at 174 billion VND","caption":"December is the peak month (VND 488.0B, 15.0% of annual spend), 2.8× February, the lowest month."},{"image":"/images/projects/itb/itb-spending-pattern.png","width":1092,"height":677,"alt":"Monthly card transactions and average ticket size, showing a sharp December transaction peak","caption":"December spending peaks because customers transact more often, not because each purchase is larger."},{"image":"/images/projects/itb/itb-eda-category-mix.png","width":2537,"height":1311,"alt":"Transactions, total spend, and average ticket by spending category","caption":"Fuel & transport is bought most often, but supermarket & grocery leads on value because its basket is 1.8× larger."},{"image":"/images/projects/itb/itb-eda-channel-mix.png","width":2353,"height":1111,"alt":"Channel share of transactions and spend, and distribution of online spend ratio","caption":"POS still carries 59% of transactions and only 21% of spend is online, even though every channel is used by at least 94% of customers."},{"image":"/images/projects/itb/itb-eda-health-distribution.png","width":2354,"height":1127,"alt":"Distribution of monthly financial health scores and share of customers scoring below 60 by month","caption":"Most customer-months are Stable (median 67.7), but 23% fall below 60, rising to 84% of customers in December."}]},"implementation":{"visuals":[{"image":"/images/projects/itb/itb-financial-health-drivers.png","width":1715,"height":834,"alt":"Financial health correlations and low-health rates across spend-to-income quintiles","caption":"Spend-to-income is the strongest financial-health signal, explaining 81% of score variance in the analysis."},{"image":"/images/projects/itb/itb-health-engagement-tension.png","width":1177,"height":636,"alt":"Profile of 106 customers who are both financially stretched and highly engaged","caption":"A group of 106 highly engaged customers is also financially stretched, indicating an opportunity for supportive interventions."},{"image":"/images/projects/itb/itb-segment-map.png","width":525,"height":520,"narrow":true,"alt":"Scatter plot of customers on financial health index versus engagement index, colored by segment, with centroids for Healthy Core, Emerging Digital, Stretched & Engaged, Low-Engagement Vulnerable, and New / Low-history","caption":"Clustering result on the health × engagement map: Healthy Core sits highest on health, Stretched & Engaged lowest, Emerging Digital furthest right on engagement, Low-Engagement Vulnerable on the left, and New / Low-history at zero engagement because they have only 1–2 months of data."},{"image":"/images/projects/itb/itb-customer-segment-profiles.png","width":1533,"height":459,"alt":"Comparison of five customer segments by financial health, stress, spending, engagement, and online spend","caption":"Five customer segments compared across health, spending pressure, engagement, and digital behavior."}],"facts":[{"label":"Financially Healthy Core · 299 · 32.4% of spend","value":"The healthiest group: a 69.3 health score (average 66.4) and only 14.5% of months below 60. Lowest spend-to-income (0.59) and credit use (0.15), with average engagement. Older (mean age 53) and more often in Engineering & IT (22.7%)."},{"label":"Emerging Digital · 184 · 31.9% of spend","value":"The youngest group (mean age 36, nobody over 55) and the highest spend per customer. Most engaged: 211 transactions a month and the most online spending. Lowest essential ratio and volatility, with above-average health (67.4)."},{"label":"Stretched & Engaged · 226 · 26.8% of spend","value":"The lowest health (62.4), with 36% of months below 60. Highest spend-to-income (0.81), credit use (0.23), and volatility, yet still in the app every day (204 transactions a month). More often in Media, arts & design (25.2%)."},{"label":"Low-Engagement Vulnerable · 199 · 8.5% of spend","value":"Fewest transactions (63 a month versus 170), fewest active days, and the lowest engagement (74.1). Health below average (65.5, 28% of months below 60) and the highest next-month crisis rate (2.1%). About 1 in 5 are financially healthy but disengaged."},{"label":"New / Low-history · 91 · Rule-based","value":"Only 1–2 observed months, so they were assigned by rule before clustering instead of being forced into a behavior cluster. About 10 transactions a month, 0.4% of spend, and mostly aged 55+ (67%)."}]},"results":{"outcomesTitle":"Key Insights","validationTitle":"Recommendations","outcomes":["One driver decides financial health: spend-to-income explains 81% of the score (ρ = −0.95), about 4× the effect of job, province, or age. Risk jumps past a threshold: tough months are almost absent below ~70% of income but reach 93% above 87%.","December overspending comes from buying more often, not buying bigger: transactions rise 2.9× while the average ticket is 2.6% smaller, pushing spend-to-income to 1.25 and 84% of customers into a tough month.","Stressed months are driven by discretionary spending, not essentials: it takes 72% of spend (vs 41% in healthy months), led by travel and online shopping.","Digital depth, not reach, is the engagement lever: 925 of 999 customers use all five channels, yet only 21% of spend is online, and online share drives engagement most (ρ = +0.83). The digital gap is generational, not regional.","Engagement and health pull apart: 106 loyal daily users (16.5% of spend) are the most stretched, while 85 financially healthy, mostly older customers barely use the card, a share-of-wallet opportunity rather than a credit risk."],"validation":["Budgeting tools: cap discretionary spend at 30% of income (50/30/20 rule) for customers who overspent at least once in the last 3 months (707 customers).","Real-time spend alerts when spend-to-income passes 0.80 or credit utilisation passes 0.70, so customers are warned before a tough month.","Year-end planning reminders: prompt a budget in October–November and send a mid-December check-in with spend to date (687 customers who spent above income in December).","Financial education: 1–2 minute in-app lessons on essential vs discretionary spending, emergency savings, and smart credit use (869 customers with at least one tough month).","Digital nudges: assisted QR/app setup and recurring-payment guidance for POS-heavy customers aged 55+ and new customers (294 customers).","Savings goals and cashback on fuel and groceries for healthy but low-use customers (53 customers), rolled out with the rest in a 10-week plan: prepare, launch financial support, launch engagement support, then monitor and decide to scale, refine, or pause."]}}},{"id":10,"slug":"airbnb-la-data-warehouse","theme":"Data Engineering","title":"Airbnb Los Angeles Data Warehouse & OLAP Analysis","category":"Data Warehousing · OLAP","description":"Every question about Los Angeles Airbnb listings started the same way: open the CSV files, clean them, and join them by hand. I moved 11,996 listings into a SQL Server data warehouse (an SSIS pipeline, a star schema, and an SSAS cube), so price, availability, and reviews can be sliced by host, location, room type, and time in one query. On top of it, three models predict which hosts are Superhosts (Random Forest: 90.19% ROC-AUC).","tags":["SQL Server","SSIS","SSAS","OLAP","Power BI","Python","scikit-learn","XGBoost"],"link":"#","github":"https://github.com/AIVIETNAM-AIO-thuhuynhtran05/AirBnB-of-Los-Angeles-Data-Warehouse-OLAP-Multidimensional-Analysis-","image":"/images/projects/airbnb-data-warehouse.svg","facts":[{"value":"11,996","label":"Listings"},{"value":"27","label":"Attributes"},{"value":"4","label":"Cube dimensions"},{"value":"90.19%","label":"Best ROC-AUC"}],"details":{"challenge":{"summary":"Raw Airbnb listing data comes as flat CSV files that cannot be queried directly: every question means opening files, cleaning them, and filtering and joining by hand before any analysis or report can start.","points":["Listing data for Los Angeles 2025 comes as flat CSV files from Inside Airbnb, with host, location, and room information mixed in one table.","Analysts need to query price, availability, and reviews across hosts, locations, room types, and time with SQL, without re-preparing the files for every question.","The project also asks which host behaviors are most associated with Superhost status."]},"solution":{"summary":"Moved the data from raw CSV files into a SQL Server data warehouse that can be queried directly with SQL, then built OLAP analysis, reporting, and data mining on top of it.","steps":["Ran EDA and transformations to produce a clean listings dataset (11,996 rows, 27 attributes).","Designed a star schema in SQL Server with a listing fact table and host, location, and room type dimensions, so the data can be queried directly with SQL.","Built an SSIS package to load the warehouse using Sort, Merge Join, Derived Column, and Multicast transformations.","Built an SSAS cube with Host, Location, Room Type, and Time dimensions, and connected a Power BI report to it.","Trained Logistic Regression, Random Forest, and XGBoost models to classify Superhosts and compared their results."]},"architecture":{"flow":["Raw Inside Airbnb CSV","EDA & cleaning","SSIS ETL","SQL Server star schema (queryable with SQL)","SSAS cube","Power BI report & data mining"],"before":["Data sits in raw CSV files that cannot be queried with SQL.","Listing, host, and location attributes are mixed together, so every question needs manual filtering and joins."],"after":["Clean data lives in a SQL Server star schema that can be queried directly with SQL for analysis.","The SSAS cube and Power BI report support reporting and drill-down across host, location, room type, and time.","Model results highlight which host behaviors are linked to Superhost status."]},"implementation":{"visuals":[{"image":"/images/projects/airbnb/airbnb-data-model.svg","width":1200,"height":590,"alt":"Data model with Fact_Listing linked to Dim_Host, Dim_RoomType, and Dim_Location, and Dim_Time linked through Dim_Host","caption":"Data model: Fact_Listing holds price, availability, and review measures, linked to the Host, Room Type, and Location dimensions. Dim_Time connects through the time_key column in Dim_Host."}],"facts":[{"label":"Data warehouse","value":"SQL Server star schema: Fact_Listing with host, location, and room type dimensions, plus a time dimension linked through hosts"},{"label":"ETL","value":"SSIS package: Sort, Merge Join, Derived Column, Multicast"},{"label":"OLAP","value":"SSAS cube with Host, Location, Room Type, and Time dimensions"},{"label":"Data mining","value":"Logistic Regression, Random Forest, and XGBoost on a 70/30 train/test split"}]},"results":{"outcomes":["Random Forest achieved the best ROC-AUC at 90.19%, XGBoost the best F1-score at 82.61%, and Logistic Regression the highest recall at 87.03%.","Response rate, review score, and acceptance rate were the most influential features for Superhost status.","Delivered a working pipeline from raw CSV to a queryable OLAP cube and Power BI report."],"validation":["SQL script, SSIS package, SSAS project, and Power BI file are available in the repository.","Model notebooks are exported as PDFs with full evaluation metrics.","Completed as a course project for IS217 (Data Warehousing and OLAP)."]}}},{"id":12,"slug":"sales-dbt-modeling","theme":"Data Engineering","status":"In progress","title":"Sales Data Transformation & Modeling with dbt","category":"Data Transformation · dbt","description":"When every report calculates sales its own way, the numbers never match. This project is turning raw sales data into clean, tested models with dbt, so every report reads from the same definitions. In progress: details and results will be added as the project develops.","tags":["dbt","SQL","Data Modeling"],"github":"https://github.com/AIVIETNAM-AIO-thuhuynhtran05/Sales-Data-Transformation-and-Modeling-with-dbt","image":"/images/projects/dbt-sales-modeling.svg"},{"id":8,"slug":"federated-credit-risk","theme":"Machine Learning","title":"Personalized Federated Heterogeneous Graph Learning for Credit Risk Prediction","category":"Federated Learning · Credit Risk","description":"Lenders cannot share customer data, so each one judges default risk from its own small slice. My graduation thesis (in progress since August 2026) tests whether they can learn together anyway: 307,511 Home Credit customers and 26.3M records become a credit graph split across 5 simulated lenders. Without moving any raw data, the federated model reached 0.7836 ROC-AUC, on par with pooling all the data (0.7858) and better than going alone at 4 of 5 lenders. A hybrid that blends the federated graph model with each lender's own LightGBM then raised the lender-average ROC-AUC from 0.7835 to 0.7908 across 3 seeds, within 0.002 of the best pooled model.","tags":["Python","PyTorch Geometric","Graph Neural Networks","Federated Learning","LightGBM","Credit Risk"],"link":"#","github":"https://github.com/AIVIETNAM-AIO-thuhuynhtran05/Personalized-Federated-Heterogeneous-Graph-Learning-for-Credit-Risk-Prediction-","image":"/images/projects/federated-credit-risk.svg","facts":[{"value":"307,511","label":"Customers"},{"value":"26.3M","label":"Credit records"},{"value":"0.7836","label":"Federated ROC-AUC"},{"value":"+0.0073","label":"Hybrid FL gain over FedAvg"}],"details":{"challenge":{"title":"The Challenge & Why This Approach","summary":"Each lender sees only its own customers, a borrower's history is a web of linked records, and lenders differ in region and size. The method combines three ideas, one for each of these problems.","points":["Why a heterogeneous graph: a credit history is relational, not one flat row. Each customer links to loans at other lenders, past applications, and monthly installment, POS, and card records (26.3M in total). A graph keeps every record and its link to the customer instead of averaging it away into summary features.","Why federated learning: lenders cannot pool raw customer data because of privacy rules and competition, yet a small lender has too little data to train a strong model alone. Federated learning shares only model weights, so lenders learn from each other while customer data stays in their own systems.","Why personalization: lenders serve different regions and range from 3.2% to 47.4% of customers, so one shared model may not fit every lender equally well. Personalized FL (fine-tuning, FedPer, Ditto) lets each lender adapt the shared model to its own customers, and a hybrid blends the shared model with a model each lender trains only on its own data."]},"solution":{"summary":"A two-branch pipeline on one shared Train / Validation / Test split (70/15/15), so centralized and federated models are scored on exactly the same 46,127 test customers.","steps":["Cleaned and checked 7 Home Credit tables, keeping orphan records (e.g. 27% of card records point to a missing previous loan) through placeholder nodes and flags instead of dropping them.","Centralized branch: aggregated the tables into 601 features for Logistic Regression and LightGBM, and built one global credit graph (6 node types, 10 edge types, 26.6M nodes) for a 2-layer HeteroGNN.","Federated branch: assigned customers to 5 simulated lenders by region (81 regions, each owned by one lender), with lender sizes drawn from Dirichlet(α = 0.5); each customer's full history moves with them.","Each lender built its own local graph, and a unified encoder was fitted from aggregate statistics only (quantiles, medians, vocabularies, sums), so all lenders share one feature space without sharing records.","Trained Local-only (no collaboration), FedAvg, and personalized FL (FedAvg + fine-tune, FedProx, FedPer, Ditto), picking checkpoints and decision thresholds on each lender's validation set.","Scored every model on each lender's test customers with 95% confidence intervals (paired bootstrap), and repeated the key runs over 3 seeds.","Measured concept shift between lenders (cross-lender scoring, per-lender calibration tests) to check how much room personalization actually has.","Built a hybrid personalized model: each lender blends the federated model with its own local LightGBM at the logit level, with the blend weight chosen on its own validation set."]},"architecture":{"title":"Pipeline & Real-world Use","flow":["7 Home Credit tables","Cleaning & orphan handling","Shared 70/15/15 split","Local credit graph per lender","Federated & personalized training","Default probability per applicant"],"beforeTitle":"Where It Applies","before":["Lender consortiums or banking groups that want a shared risk model but cannot exchange customer records, such as banks, consumer-finance companies, and fintech lenders in one market.","Smaller or newly expanding lenders with too few defaults to train on alone: in the experiments, two lenders with about 10% of customers each gained +0.03 ROC-AUC from collaborating.","Credit bureaus or industry bodies coordinating a common model while each member keeps its data on its own servers."],"afterTitle":"How a Lender Would Use It","after":["Each lender runs a training client on its own infrastructure; a coordinator only averages model weights each round.","When a loan application arrives, the lender builds the applicant's graph from its own records and the model returns a default probability.","The probability supports the credit decision: approve low-risk applicants, send borderline cases to manual review, and decline or re-price high-risk ones, with each lender setting its own threshold.","The model is retrained as new repayment data comes in, again without moving raw data."]},"implementation":{"visuals":[{"image":"/images/projects/federated/fed-pipeline.svg","width":1200,"height":1068,"alt":"Two-branch pipeline: shared data preparation and split, a centralized branch with tabular and graph models, and a federated branch with five lenders that ends in a hybrid step blending the federated model with each lender's own LightGBM, both meeting in a shared comparison","caption":"Pipeline: both branches share one split, so every model is compared on the same test customers. The centralized branch is the upper bound; the federated branch never moves raw records between lenders."}],"facts":[{"label":"Dataset","value":"Home Credit · 7 tables · 307,511 customers · 26.3M records"},{"label":"Graph","value":"Customer, bureau loan, previous loan, installment, POS, and card nodes · 26.6M nodes"},{"label":"Model","value":"HeteroGNN (2-layer SAGEConv) in PyTorch Geometric"},{"label":"Federation","value":"5 simulated lenders · region-based split · Dirichlet(α = 0.5) sizes"},{"label":"FL methods","value":"Local-only, FedAvg, FedAvg + fine-tune, FedProx, FedPer, Ditto, hybrid (federated + local LightGBM)"},{"label":"Baselines","value":"Logistic Regression, LightGBM (601 features), centralized HeteroGNN"}]},"results":{"outcomesTitle":"Model Output","validationTitle":"Key Findings","outcomes":["For each applicant, the lender's model returns a probability of default, for example 0.02 for a low-risk applicant and 0.21 for a high-risk one.","Each lender turns that probability into a flag using its own threshold, chosen on its validation set (0.16–0.22 across lenders).","At these thresholds, the FedAvg model flags 13% of test applicants and catches 45% of actual defaulters; flagged applicants default at 27%, 3.4× the 8.1% base rate.","Every model is reported by ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, KS, and F1 per lender, plus the lender average and the worst lender."],"validation":["Collaboration pays off: FedAvg beat Local-only at all 5 lenders in each of 3 seeds (+0.017 ROC-AUC on average, up to +0.03), significant at 4 of 5 lenders by paired bootstrap.","Privacy costs almost nothing: FedAvg reached 0.7836 ROC-AUC vs 0.7858 for the centralized HeteroGNN, with no significant difference at any lender.","Personalizing the graph model alone added no clear gain: no method beat FedAvg significantly. Lenders split by region share almost the same risk patterns, so FedAvg was already close to the centralized upper bound.","The hybrid did: blending FedAvg with each lender's local LightGBM lifted the lender-average ROC-AUC from 0.7835 to 0.7908 (+0.0073, positive in all 3 seeds), the best federated result and within 0.002 of centralized LightGBM (0.7928).","Most of that gain comes from combining graph and tabular views of the customer, so next steps are testing personalization on lenders that differ more strongly and tuning the HeteroGNN."]}}},{"id":13,"slug":"vin-datathon-sales-forecasting","theme":"Machine Learning","title":"VinDatathon 2026 – Sales Forecasting","category":"Time Series Forecasting · Retail","description":"After six years of decline, the business has to plan stock, promotions, and logistics without knowing next year's sales. Part 4 of the VinDatathon project forecasts daily Revenue and COGS for the next 548 days (01/01/2023 – 01/07/2024) from 10.5 years of history. An ensemble of Ridge and LightGBM, built only from calendar features, reached R² 0.807 on 2022 and cut daily error by 37% against a naive baseline. It shows revenue holding flat at about 1.18B VND in 2023, with no return to the 2014–18 level.","tags":["Python","LightGBM","Ridge","Prophet","SHAP","scikit-learn","Time Series Forecasting"],"link":"#","github":"https://github.com/AIVIETNAM-AIO-thuhuynhtran05/Vin-Datathon","image":"/images/projects/vin-sales-forecasting.svg","facts":[{"value":"3,833","label":"Days of sales history"},{"value":"548","label":"Days to forecast"},{"value":"81","label":"Calendar features"},{"value":"3","label":"Time-based validation folds"}],"details":{"challenge":{"label":"The Problem","title":"Why This Forecast Is Hard","summary":"The business needs daily Revenue and COGS from January 2023 to July 2024 to plan stock, promotions, and logistics. The competition scores the forecast on MAE, RMSE, and R², and disqualifies the whole part for using test values, using outside data, or results that cannot be reproduced. Three things make it hard.","points":["<strong>The horizon is 18 months long.</strong> There is no yesterday or last week to learn from, so the usual lag features cannot be used. Every input has to be known in advance from the calendar.","<strong>The level keeps shifting.</strong> Average daily revenue was 5.27M VND in 2014–18 but 2.98M in 2020–22, 43.5% lower. A model trained on all years averages the eras and lands at a level that fits none of them.","<strong>There is no feedback from the test period.</strong> Every choice of model, weight, and correction has to be justified by validation on past years, not by trial and error on the leaderboard."]},"solution":{"label":"Approach","title":"The Steps and Why Each One Matters","summary":"Nine steps, each answering a question the previous one raised. Every pattern found in the data turns into a feature or a design decision, and every model choice is made on validation, not by hand.","steps":["<strong>Read the rules first.</strong> An 18-month horizon, no test values, no outside data, and fixed random seeds.<br><em>Why it matters:</em> these rules decide the whole design: no lag features, and every input must be computable for 2023–2024 ahead of time.","<strong>Explore the series.</strong> Split the history into four eras and check the seasonal shape, month-end peaks, Lunar New Year (Tết), odd and even years, and margin by quarter.<br><em>Why it matters:</em> each pattern found becomes a feature or a modeling decision, so nothing later is guesswork.","<strong>Build features from the calendar only.</strong> 81 features: date parts, start and end of month, Fourier seasonality, Tết distance, public holidays, odd-year flag, and a promotion calendar derived from the repeating pattern in promotions.csv.<br><em>Why it matters:</em> the features for 2023–2024 can be built without any future sales, which rules out leakage at the source.","<strong>Validate the way the forecast will be used.</strong> Three time-based folds (validate on 2022, on 2021, and on July 2021 – June 2022), a seasonal naive baseline, and a \"shape\" error that rescales each forecast to the true total.<br><em>Why it matters:</em> each fold forecasts a future year from the past, and the shape error separates \"peaks in the wrong place\" from \"the whole year too high or too low\".","<strong>Train three different model families.</strong> Ridge on Fourier terms as a stable anchor, LightGBM weighted toward the clean 2014–18 era, four LightGBM specialists that each focus on one quarter, and Prophet on the recent years.<br><em>Why it matters:</em> models that fail in different places can correct each other when combined.","<strong>Let validation choose the blend.</strong> A grid search over weights picked 0.3 Ridge + 0.7 quarter specialists and gave Prophet zero weight.<br><em>Why it matters:</em> the data, not preference, decides which model to trust; the blend beats the best single model by 3.5% on shape error.","<strong>Correct the level separately.</strong> Multiply by c = actual 2022 total ÷ forecast 2022 total (1.08 for revenue).<br><em>Why it matters:</em> the model learns the shape from 2014–18 but runs 4–7% low on level; the factor comes from training data only, not from leaderboard tuning.","<strong>Explain the forecast with SHAP.</strong> TreeSHAP on the LightGBM model, grouped into business drivers.<br><em>Why it matters:</em> the business can see why a day is forecast high or low, not just the number.","<strong>Produce and check the submission.</strong> 548 rows of Date, Revenue, COGS with no negatives or gaps, reproducible with random_state 42.<br><em>Why it matters:</em> a forecast that breaks the format or cannot be rerun scores zero."]},"architecture":{"label":"Pipeline","title":"From Data Patterns to Design Decisions","flow":["sales.csv · 3,833 days","Time-series EDA","81 calendar features","3 time-based folds","Ridge + LightGBM + Prophet","Blend + level calibration","SHAP + 548-day forecast"],"beforeTitle":"What the data showed","before":["Four eras with very different levels: 4.39M VND a day in 2012–13, 5.27M in 2014–18, 3.12M in 2019, and 2.98M in 2020–22.","Every year has the same shape: low in January and February, highest from April to June, falling toward December.","The last 3 days of a month average 7.17M VND, double the 3.58M of mid-month days.","Two promotions, including a 50% Urban Blowout in late July, run only in odd years. In odd years, Q3 COGS is 1.04× revenue (sold at a loss), versus 0.86× in even years.","COGS tracks revenue at about 0.85, but 382 days have COGS above revenue."],"afterTitle":"What the pipeline does about it","after":["Weight 2014–18 fully and other years at 1% so the trees learn a clean seasonal shape, then fix the level with a separate calibration step.","Model the yearly shape with Fourier terms (5 harmonics), so the same curve carries into 2023 and 2024.","Add start- and end-of-month flags and days to month-end.","Add an odd-year flag and quarter specialists, so 2023 (odd) and 2024 (even) get different third quarters.","Forecast COGS with its own models instead of a fixed share of revenue."]},"eda":{"label":"The Data","title":"What 10.5 Years of Daily Sales Show","summary":"The only target is sales.csv: daily Revenue and COGS from 04/07/2012 to 31/12/2022, 3,833 days with no gaps. Other tables (orders, web traffic, inventory) stop at the end of 2022, so they cannot describe the forecast period; they are used only to read the repeating promotion calendar.","visuals":[{"image":"/images/projects/vin-forecast/fc-eras.png","width":2000,"height":1080,"alt":"Line chart of 30-day average daily revenue from 2012 to 2022 in four colored eras: 2012–13 averaging 4.39M VND a day, 2014–18 averaging 5.27M with high seasonal peaks, 2019 at 3.12M, and 2020–22 at 2.98M","caption":"The seasonal shape repeats every year, but the level dropped in 2019 and never came back. This is why the model learns the shape and the level separately."}],"facts":[{"label":"Seasonality","value":"Low in January and February, peaking from April to June, then easing toward December. The shape is stable across years; only the level changes."},{"label":"Month-end","value":"The last 3 days of a month average 7.17M VND, the first 3 days 5.12M, and mid-month days 3.58M."},{"label":"Lunar New Year","value":"Revenue averages 2.70M a day in the month before Tết, 3.30M around it, and 3.91M three to four weeks after."},{"label":"Odd and even years","value":"Two campaigns run only in odd years. August averages 5.50M a day in even years and 3.25M in odd years."}]},"implementation":{"label":"Models & Validation","title":"How Well It Works and What Drives It","summary":"Every model was trained on log revenue and scored on three folds that each forecast a full year from the past. LightGBM beat the naive baseline in every fold. Prophet did worse than the baseline because it only learns from the last two to three years, so the blend dropped it.","visuals":[{"image":"/images/projects/vin-forecast/fc-cv.png","width":2000,"height":1080,"alt":"Bar chart of mean absolute error on revenue averaged over three folds: ensemble 545K VND a day (R² 0.80), LightGBM quarter specialists 554K, LightGBM 558K, Ridge 645K, seasonal naive 841K (R² 0.45), Prophet 965K","caption":"Validation error by model. On 2022 alone, the calibrated ensemble reached MAE 544K VND a day and R² 0.807, against 864K and 0.445 for the naive baseline."},{"image":"/images/projects/vin-forecast/fc-shap.png","width":2000,"height":1080,"alt":"Bar chart of SHAP importance by driver group: season of the year 58.7%, era level and trend 25.4%, start and end of month 6.3%, day of week 6.0%, promotion campaigns 2.6%, Tết 0.6%, odd or even year 0.3%, holidays 0%","caption":"What drives the forecast: the time of year and the era level explain 84%. Promotions add only 2.6%, which matches the finding that peaks are seasonal, not promotional. On the last day of a month, the month-end features alone lift the forecast 29% above mid-month."},{"image":"/images/projects/vin-forecast/fc-forecast.png","width":2000,"height":1080,"alt":"Line chart of actual daily revenue for 2021–2022 in grey and the forecast from January 2023 to July 2024 in blue, repeating the April-to-June peak and month-end spikes","caption":"The forecast repeats the April-to-June peak and the month-end spikes at about the 2022 level."}],"facts":[{"label":"Ridge + Fourier","value":"Linear model on standardized calendar features. Weaker alone (MAE 645K) but its errors only correlate 0.70 with LightGBM's, so it keeps 30% of the blend."},{"label":"LightGBM, quarter specialists","value":"Four models, each weighting its own quarter twice. Each day takes the forecast of its quarter's specialist. Best single model (MAE 554K, R² 0.80)."},{"label":"Level calibration","value":"c = 1.08 for revenue and 1.10 for COGS. Tested honestly: a factor estimated on 2021 and applied to 2022 lowered MAE from 567K to 544K."},{"label":"Leakage control","value":"Features come from dates only; the promotion calendar is derived from promotions.csv; Tết and holiday dates are calendar knowledge, not outside data."}]},"results":{"outcomesTitle":"What the Forecast Says","validationTitle":"What to Do With It","outcomes":["Revenue holds flat: 1.18B VND forecast for 2023 against 1.17B in 2022, and 0.71B for the first half of 2024 against 0.69B a year and a half earlier. There is no sign of a return to the 2014–18 level.","April to June bring 37.9% of 2023 revenue.","Q3 2023 is forecast to sell at a loss, with COGS at 1.05× revenue (0.89× in Q3 2022). It falls in an odd year, when the 50% Urban Blowout campaign runs.","Month-end stays the short-term peak: the last 3 days of a month are forecast at 5.55M VND a day, nearly double mid-month (2.92M).","Typical daily error is about 0.54M VND, around 17% of an average 2022 day, so the forecast is reliable for monthly and quarterly plans and a guide for single days."],"validation":["Plan stock, staff, and delivery capacity for April to June from March, since that quarter carries the year.","Review the depth of the odd-year Urban Blowout discount before July 2023, as it lines up with a forecast loss in Q3.","Time campaigns and replenishment around month-end, when demand reliably doubles.","Do not count on promotions to grow revenue: they explain only 2.6% of the forecast, so growth has to come from conversion and retention (see the analytics project).","Next steps: check the row order against the official sample submission, score on the leaderboard, and retest Prophet with a longer window."]}},"related":{"label":"Parts 1–3 and 5 of the project","title":"E-commerce Business Analytics","text":"The same project explains why the business declined, who brings the revenue, and what to fix first: data quality checks, a five-page Power BI dashboard, and customer segmentation.","href":"/projects/vin-datathon","cta":"See the analytics project"}}]